/* C1 · topics 6–10 (format: see a2-part1.js). */
module.exports = [
  { title: 'Culture and identity', lessons: [
    { t: ['Identity and belonging', 'Shaxsiyat va mansublik'],
      obj: ['Shaxsiy, madaniy va ijtimoiy identifikatsiyani chuqur tahlil qilasiz.', 'You will analyse personal, cultural and social identity in depth.'],
      ex: ['Identifikatsiya: identity, belonging (mansublik), heritage, roots, ethnicity, nationality, diaspora (diaspora), assimilation vs integration, hybrid identity, sense of community. Ibora: feel at home, a sense of belonging, strike a balance between…, be torn between… (ikki o‘rtasida qolmoq). Nisbiy gap kengaytmasi: a community whose members share…\nIfoda: Identity is shaped by (… tomonidan shakllanadi). It is not so much A as B (A emas, ko‘proq B). Not only…, but also… (inversiya). Fikr: It could be argued that identity is fluid rather than fixed.', 'Identity vocabulary: identity, belonging, heritage, roots, ethnicity, nationality, diaspora, assimilation vs integration, hybrid identity, sense of community. Phrases: feel at home, a sense of belonging, strike a balance between…, be torn between… Extended relative clause: a community whose members share…\nExpressions: Identity is shaped by… It is not so much A as B. Not only…, but also… (inversion). Opinion: It could be argued that identity is fluid rather than fixed.'],
      eg: [['Identity is shaped by family, language and experience.', 'Shaxsiyat oila, til va tajriba bilan shakllanadi.'], ['She is torn between two cultures.', 'U ikki madaniyat orasida qolgan.'], ['It is not so much where you live as how you feel.', 'Muhimi qayerda yashashing emas, qanday his qilishing.'], ['The diaspora keeps its traditions alive.', 'Diaspora o‘z an’analarini saqlab keladi.']],
      w: [['belonging', 'mansublik hissi', 'принадлежность', 'noun', 'A sense of belonging is essential.', 'Mansublik hissi zarur.', 'bɪˈlɒŋɪŋ'], ['diaspora', 'diaspora', 'диаспора', 'noun', 'The diaspora lives across Europe.', 'Diaspora Yevropa bo‘ylab yashaydi.', 'daɪˈæspərə'], ['assimilation', 'singib ketish (o‘z o‘zligini yo‘qotib)', 'ассимиляция', 'noun', 'Assimilation can erase traditions.', 'Singib ketish an’analarni yo‘q qilishi mumkin.', 'əˌsɪməˈleɪʃn'], ['ethnicity', 'etnik mansublik', 'этническая принадлежность', 'noun', 'Ethnicity is part of identity.', 'Etnik mansublik shaxsiyatning bir qismi.', 'eθˈnɪsəti'], ['hybrid', 'aralash', 'гибридный', 'adjective', 'She has a hybrid identity.', 'Uning aralash shaxsiyati bor.', 'ˈhaɪbrɪd'], ['roots', 'ildizlar', 'корни', 'noun', 'He returned to his roots.', 'U ildizlariga qaytdi.', 'ruːts']],
      g: [['It is not so much where you live ___ how you feel.', 'as', 'than', 'but', '“not so much A as B”.'], ['Not only ___ identity shape behaviour, but it also affects choices.', 'does', 'is', 'has', 'Inversiya.']],
      dlg: ['Interviewer: Do you feel more Uzbek or more British? Dilnoza: It is not so much a matter of either as of both. I am torn at times. Interviewer: How do you keep your roots? Dilnoza: Through language, food and family traditions. Interviewer: And belonging? Dilnoza: Belonging comes from community, not just a passport.', 'Where does belonging come from, according to Dilnoza?', 'Community', 'A passport', 'Money', 'She says “Belonging comes from community”.'],
      rd: ['Identity is not a single label but a combination of factors: nationality, language, religion, profession and personal values. For migrants and their children, these factors can pull in different directions, leading to what sociologists call hybrid identity. Some people assimilate, adopting the culture of the majority, while others integrate, retaining their heritage while participating fully in society. Research suggests that those who maintain a strong connection to their roots while building new relationships tend to report a greater sense of belonging.', ['Who report a greater sense of belonging?', 'Those who keep their roots and build new relationships', 'Those who reject their heritage', 'Those who avoid society'], ['Identity is a single label.', 'False'], ['The sociologists were interviewed in 2019.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Write an essay paragraph (150 words): “Identity is shaped more by culture than by place.” Discuss with examples.', 'it could be argued|not so much|shaped by|sense of belonging', 110, 'It could be argued that identity is shaped more by culture than by place. It is not so much where people live as how they feel connected to a community. Language, food and traditions create a sense of belonging even for members of a diaspora. Not only do these elements preserve roots, but they also help people integrate into new societies.'],
      sp: ['Describe what gives you a sense of belonging.', 'Belonging for me comes from family traditions and language. It is not so much a place as a feeling.'],
      ts: [['A population living outside its homeland:', 'diaspora', 'heritage', 'ethnicity'], ['“Mansublik” in English:', 'belonging', 'assimilation', 'hybrid'], ['She is ___ between two cultures.', 'torn', 'tore', 'tearing']] },
    { t: ['Language and culture', 'Til va madaniyat'],
      obj: ['Til va madaniyat o‘rtasidagi aloqani tahlil qilasiz.', 'You will analyse the relationship between language and culture.'],
      ex: ['Til va madaniyat: mother tongue, bilingualism, language loss (til yo‘qolishi), endangered language, dialect, accent, lingua franca (umumiy muloqot tili), linguistic diversity, translation loss. Sapir–Whorf gipotezasi: til fikrlashni shakllantirishi mumkin (bahsli). Kuchaytirish ifodalari: arguably, to some degree, by no means (aslo emas).\nInversiya: Rarely do translations capture every nuance. Little did he realise… Qisqa ibora: A case in point is… (misol). Kelasi tuzilma: be set to + fe’l, be bound to.', 'Language and culture: mother tongue, bilingualism, language loss, endangered language, dialect, accent, lingua franca, linguistic diversity, translation loss. Sapir–Whorf hypothesis: language may shape thought (debated). Emphasis: arguably, to some degree, by no means.\nInversion: Rarely do translations capture every nuance. Little did he realise… Example phrase: A case in point is… Structures: be set to + verb, be bound to.'],
      eg: [['Rarely do translations capture every nuance.', 'Tarjimalar kamdan-kam har bir nozik ma’noni aks ettiradi.'], ['English has become a lingua franca in business.', 'Ingliz tili biznesda umumiy muloqot tiliga aylangan.'], ['A case in point is the loss of minority languages.', 'Misol — ozchilik tillarining yo‘qolishi.'], ['Bilingualism is by no means a disadvantage.', 'Ikki tillilik aslo kamchilik emas.']],
      w: [['bilingual', 'ikki tilli', 'двуязычный', 'adjective', 'She grew up bilingual.', 'U ikki tilli bo‘lib o‘sgan.', 'baɪˈlɪŋɡwəl'], ['lingua franca', 'umumiy muloqot tili', 'лингва франка', 'noun', 'English is a lingua franca.', 'Ingliz tili umumiy muloqot tili.', 'ˌlɪŋɡwə ˈfræŋkə'], ['dialect', 'lahja', 'диалект', 'noun', 'Each region has its dialect.', 'Har bir mintaqaning o‘z lahjasi bor.', 'ˈdaɪəlekt'], ['endangered', 'yo‘qolib borayotgan', 'находящийся под угрозой', 'adjective', 'Many languages are endangered.', 'Ko‘p tillar yo‘qolib bormoqda.', 'ɪnˈdeɪndʒəd'], ['nuance', 'nozik ma’no', 'нюанс', 'noun', 'The translation misses the nuance.', 'Tarjima nozik ma’noni o‘tkazib yuboradi.', 'ˈnjuːɑːns'], ['mother tongue', 'ona tili', 'родной язык', 'noun', 'Her mother tongue is Uzbek.', 'Uning ona tili — o‘zbek tili.', 'ˈmʌðə tʌŋ']],
      g: [['Rarely ___ translations capture every nuance.', 'do', 'are', 'have', 'Inversiya.'], ['Bilingualism is by ___ means a disadvantage.', 'no', 'not', 'any', '“by no means”.']],
      dlg: ['Linguist: Around half of the world’s languages are endangered. Student: Why does it matter? Linguist: Each language carries a unique view of the world. Student: But a lingua franca makes communication easier. Linguist: By all means, but rarely do translations capture every cultural nuance.', 'Why do endangered languages matter, according to the linguist?', 'Each carries a unique view of the world', 'They are easy to learn', 'They are used in business', 'She says “a unique view of the world”.'],
      rd: ['Languages are more than tools for communication; they reflect the history and values of those who speak them. Linguists estimate that, without intervention, nearly half of the world’s roughly 7,000 languages will disappear by the end of the century. When a language dies, much of its oral literature, local knowledge and cultural memory may disappear with it. Some communities have responded with immersion schools and digital dictionaries, which suggests that endangered languages can be revitalised when speakers have both motivation and institutional support.', ['What can help revitalise a language?', 'Motivation and institutional support', 'Banning the language', 'Ignoring speakers'], ['Languages are only communication tools.', 'False'], ['Immersion schools were first opened in Wales.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Write an argumentative paragraph (150 words) on whether linguistic diversity should be protected.', 'a case in point|rarely do|by no means|arguably', 110, 'Linguistic diversity should arguably be protected. A case in point is the loss of minority languages, which carry unique cultural knowledge. Rarely do translations capture every nuance of such knowledge. Bilingualism is by no means a burden; on the contrary, research suggests it improves cognitive flexibility. Therefore, education systems should support mother tongues alongside lingua francas.'],
      sp: ['Say what your mother tongue means to you.', 'My mother tongue is part of my identity. It connects me with my family and my culture.'],
      ts: [['A common language between speakers of different languages:', 'lingua franca', 'dialect', 'accent'], ['“Ona tili” in English:', 'mother tongue', 'dialect', 'nuance'], ['Little ___ he realise the consequences.', 'did', 'was', 'had']] },
    { t: ['Cross-cultural communication', 'Madaniyatlararo muloqot'],
      obj: ['Madaniyatlararo muloqotdagi to‘siqlar va strategiyalarni muhokama qilasiz.', 'You will discuss barriers and strategies in cross-cultural communication.'],
      ex: ['Madaniy o‘lchamlar (Hofstede): individualism vs collectivism, high- vs low-context communication (yuqori/past kontekst), power distance (hokimiyat masofasi), uncertainty avoidance. Xato manbalari: stereotypes, ethnocentrism (etnotsentrizm), cultural assumptions. Strategiyalar: active listening, empathy, adaptability, asking clarifying questions.\nIboralar: In high-context cultures, much is left unsaid. What may appear rude in one culture is considered polite in another. Taqqoslash: whereas, in contrast to, as opposed to. Kuchaytirish: What matters most is…',  'Cultural dimensions (Hofstede): individualism vs collectivism, high- vs low-context communication, power distance, uncertainty avoidance. Sources of misunderstanding: stereotypes, ethnocentrism, cultural assumptions. Strategies: active listening, empathy, adaptability, asking clarifying questions.\nPhrases: In high-context cultures, much is left unsaid. What may appear rude in one culture is considered polite in another. Comparison: whereas, in contrast to, as opposed to. Emphasis: What matters most is…'],
      eg: [['In high-context cultures, much is left unsaid.', 'Yuqori kontekstli madaniyatlarda ko‘p narsa aytilmay qoladi.'], ['What matters most is willingness to listen.', 'Eng muhimi — tinglashga tayyorlik.'], ['As opposed to direct cultures, others value indirectness.', 'To‘g‘ridan-to‘g‘ri madaniyatlardan farqli ravishda, boshqalar bilvosita ifodani qadrlaydi.'], ['Stereotypes can distort first impressions.', 'Stereotiplar birinchi taassurotni buzishi mumkin.']],
      w: [['stereotype', 'stereotip', 'стереотип', 'noun', 'Avoid cultural stereotypes.', 'Madaniy stereotiplardan saqlaning.', 'ˈsteriətaɪp'], ['ethnocentrism', 'etnotsentrizm', 'этноцентризм', 'noun', 'Ethnocentrism limits understanding.', 'Etnotsentrizm tushunishni cheklaydi.', 'ˌeθnəʊˈsentrɪzəm'], ['empathy', 'hamdardlik, anglash', 'эмпатия', 'noun', 'Empathy improves communication.', 'Hamdardlik muloqotni yaxshilaydi.', 'ˈempəθi'], ['collectivism', 'jamoaviylik', 'коллективизм', 'noun', 'Collectivism values group harmony.', 'Jamoaviylik guruh uyg‘unligini qadrlaydi.', 'kəˈlektɪvɪzəm'], ['indirect', 'bilvosita', 'косвенный', 'adjective', 'Indirect refusals are polite.', 'Bilvosita rad etish muloyim.', 'ˌɪndəˈrekt'], ['adaptability', 'moslashuvchanlik', 'адаптивность', 'noun', 'Adaptability is a key skill.', 'Moslashuvchanlik asosiy ko‘nikma.', 'əˌdæptəˈbɪləti']],
      g: [['In high-context cultures, much ___ unsaid.', 'is left', 'leaves', 'is leaving', 'Majhul.'], ['What matters ___ is willingness to listen.', 'most', 'mostly', 'the more', 'Cleft.']],
      dlg: ['Trainer: Why did the meeting go badly? Manager: My colleague stayed silent when I asked for his opinion. Trainer: In some cultures, silence signals respect. Manager: So I misread it. Trainer: What matters most is empathy and asking clarifying questions rather than assuming.', 'What does the trainer say matters most?', 'Empathy and clarifying questions', 'Speaking louder', 'Avoiding meetings', 'She says “empathy and asking clarifying questions”.'],
      rd: ['Globalisation has made cross-cultural communication a daily reality for millions of workers. Misunderstandings often arise not from language but from differing assumptions. In low-context cultures, messages are explicit and direct; in high-context cultures, meaning depends on shared understanding, tone and silence. Individualistic societies value independence and personal opinions, whereas collectivist ones emphasise group harmony. Training programmes therefore encourage employees to question their own assumptions, listen actively and adapt their style to the situation.', ['What do training programmes encourage?', 'Questioning assumptions and adapting style', 'Avoiding foreign colleagues', 'Using only one style'], ['Misunderstandings come only from vocabulary.', 'False'], ['Training lasts one year.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Write a short case study (180 words) of a cross-cultural misunderstanding and how it could be resolved.', 'high-context|assumption|what matters most|as opposed to', 120, 'A manager from a low-context culture asked a colleague for direct feedback, but the colleague stayed silent. In high-context cultures, silence may show respect rather than disagreement. The manager assumed that the colleague had no opinion, which was a cultural assumption. What matters most is empathy and asking clarifying questions. As opposed to making assumptions, the manager should adapt and listen.'],
      sp: ['Describe a cultural difference you have noticed and how to handle it.', 'In some cultures people speak indirectly. What matters most is to listen carefully and ask questions.'],
      ts: [['Believing your culture is superior:', 'ethnocentrism', 'empathy', 'adaptability'], ['“Bilvosita” in English:', 'indirect', 'collective', 'hybrid'], ['Avoid cultural ___.', 'stereotypes', 'stereotype of', 'stereotyping to']] },
    { t: ['A culture essay', 'Madaniyat haqida esse'],
      obj: ['Madaniyat va shaxsiyat mavzusida murakkab, chuqur tahliliy esse yozasiz.', 'You will write a complex, analytical essay on culture and identity.'],
      ex: ['Tahliliy esse: nuance (nozik ma’no), evaluation, synthesis (sintez), counterpoint. Tuzilma: Introduction (hook, definition of key terms, thesis) → Body (analysis + evaluation + examples) → Conclusion (synthesis, wider implications). Kalit atamalarni aniqlash: By “culture”, this essay refers to… Ta’sirchan o‘tish: Having established…, it is now necessary to consider…\nUslub: nominalizatsiya, hedging, kohezion vositalar (such, this, the latter), kompleks gaplar. Aniq misollar: ismlar, sanalar, raqamlar. Xulosa: Ultimately, … ; The wider implication is that…',  'Analytical essay: nuance, evaluation, synthesis, counterpoint. Structure: Introduction (hook, definition of key terms, thesis) → Body (analysis + evaluation + examples) → Conclusion (synthesis, wider implications). Defining terms: By “culture”, this essay refers to… Transitions: Having established…, it is now necessary to consider…\nStyle: nominalisation, hedging, cohesive devices (such, this, the latter), complex sentences. Concrete examples: names, dates, figures. Conclusion: Ultimately, … ; The wider implication is that…'],
      eg: [['By “culture”, this essay refers to shared values and practices.', 'Bu esseda “madaniyat” deganda umumiy qadriyat va amaliyotlar nazarda tutiladi.'], ['Having established the definition, it is necessary to consider its limits.', 'Ta’rifni aniqlab, uning chegaralarini ko‘rib chiqish kerak.'], ['Ultimately, culture is dynamic rather than static.', 'Oxir-oqibat madaniyat statik emas, dinamik.'], ['The wider implication is that policies must be flexible.', 'Kengroq oqibat — siyosatlar moslashuvchan bo‘lishi kerak.']],
      w: [['dynamic', 'dinamik, o‘zgaruvchan', 'динамичный', 'adjective', 'Culture is dynamic.', 'Madaniyat dinamik.', 'daɪˈnæmɪk'], ['synthesis', 'sintez', 'синтез', 'noun', 'The conclusion offers a synthesis.', 'Xulosa sintez beradi.', 'ˈsɪnθəsɪs'], ['static', 'o‘zgarmas', 'статичный', 'adjective', 'Cultures are not static.', 'Madaniyatlar o‘zgarmas emas.', 'ˈstætɪk'], ['definition', 'ta’rif', 'определение', 'noun', 'Give a clear definition.', 'Aniq ta’rif bering.', 'ˌdefɪˈnɪʃn'], ['counterpoint', 'qarama-qarshi fikr', 'контраргумент', 'noun', 'Include a counterpoint.', 'Qarama-qarshi fikrni qo‘shing.', 'ˈkaʊntəpɔɪnt'], ['ultimately', 'oxir-oqibat', 'в конечном счёте', 'adverb', 'Ultimately, the choice is personal.', 'Oxir-oqibat tanlov shaxsiy.', 'ˈʌltɪmətli']],
      g: [['___ established the definition, it is necessary to consider its limits.', 'Having', 'Have', 'Had been to', 'Perfect participle.'], ['Cultures are not ___ ; they evolve.', 'static', 'statically', 'stat', 'Sifat kerak.']],
      dlg: ['Supervisor: Your introduction doesn’t define “culture”. Student: I assumed everyone knew. Supervisor: In analytical essays you should state: By “culture”, this essay refers to… Student: And the conclusion? Supervisor: Offer a synthesis and say what the wider implication is.', 'What should the introduction include?', 'A definition of key terms', 'A list of references', 'A joke', 'The supervisor says to define the key terms.'],
      rd: ['Culture is often treated as a fixed set of traditions, yet anthropologists increasingly describe it as dynamic: a continuous negotiation between the past and the present. Food, clothing and language all change as societies interact. Critics of globalisation fear the loss of local traditions, whereas supporters emphasise that exchange enriches cultures. A balanced view may be that cultures survive not by resisting change but by absorbing it selectively, preserving what is meaningful and adapting what is not.', ['How do cultures survive, according to the balanced view?', 'By absorbing change selectively', 'By resisting all change', 'By copying others'], ['Anthropologists describe culture as fixed.', 'False'], ['Globalisation began in the 20th century.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Write an analytical essay (300 words): “Globalisation threatens cultural identity.” To what extent do you agree?', 'by “culture”|having established|ultimately|the wider implication', 210, 'By “cultural identity”, this essay refers to the shared values, language and practices that define a community. It is often argued that globalisation threatens these. Admittedly, global brands can displace local traditions. Having established this risk, it is necessary to consider the counterpoint: globalisation also enables communities to share and preserve their heritage online. Ultimately, culture is dynamic rather than static. The wider implication is that policy should support local culture while remaining open.'],
      sp: ['Give a 90-second analytical answer: is culture more likely to be preserved or changed by globalisation?', 'Culture is dynamic, so it will change. However, communities can preserve what is meaningful through education and media.'],
      ts: [['Not changing:', 'static', 'dynamic', 'hybrid'], ['“Sintez” in English:', 'synthesis', 'definition', 'counterpoint'], ['___ , culture evolves.', 'Ultimately', 'Ultimate', 'Ultimateness']] },
  ], exam: [['Rarely ___ translations capture all nuance.', 'do', 'are', 'did to', 'grammar'], ['Not only ___ identity shape behaviour, but also choices.', 'does', 'is', 'has', 'grammar'], ['Belief that your own culture is superior:', 'ethnocentrism', 'collectivism', 'empathy', 'vocabulary']] },

  { title: 'Language and communication', lessons: [
    { t: ['Body language and pragmatics', 'Tana tili va pragmatika'],
      obj: ['Tana tili va pragmatik ma’noni (nutq maqsadini) tushunib, to‘g‘ri ishlatasiz.', 'You will understand and use body language and pragmatic meaning appropriately.'],
      ex: ['Pragmatika — gap ma’nosi kontekstga bog‘liqligi. Nutq akti: request (so‘rov), refusal, apology, compliment. Bilvosita so‘rov: Could you possibly…?, I was wondering if you’d mind… Tana tili: posture (qomat), gestures, eye contact, facial expressions, personal space, proxemics. Madaniy farq: nodding does not always mean agreement.\nImplicature: “It’s cold in here” = please close the window. Hedging va yumshatish: I’m afraid I can’t…, With all due respect,… Paralanguage: tone, pitch, pace, pauses.', 'Pragmatics studies how meaning depends on context. Speech acts: request, refusal, apology, compliment. Indirect requests: Could you possibly…?, I was wondering if you’d mind… Body language: posture, gestures, eye contact, facial expressions, personal space (proxemics). Cultural difference: nodding does not always mean agreement.\nImplicature: “It’s cold in here” = please close the window. Softeners: I’m afraid I can’t…, With all due respect,… Paralanguage: tone, pitch, pace, pauses.'],
      eg: [['I was wondering if you’d mind opening the window.', 'Derazani ochsangiz bo‘ladimi, deb o‘ylagan edim.'], ['“It’s cold in here” may be a request.', '“Bu yerda sovuq” so‘rov bo‘lishi mumkin.'], ['Eye contact has different meanings across cultures.', 'Ko‘z bilan aloqa madaniyatlarda turlicha ma’no beradi.'], ['With all due respect, I disagree.', 'Hurmat bilan, men rozi emasman.']],
      w: [['pragmatics', 'pragmatika', 'прагматика', 'noun', 'Pragmatics studies meaning in context.', 'Pragmatika kontekstdagi ma’noni o‘rganadi.', 'præɡˈmætɪks'], ['implicature', 'yashirin ma’no', 'импликатура', 'noun', 'The implicature was clear.', 'Yashirin ma’no aniq edi.', 'ˈɪmplɪkətʃə'], ['gesture', 'ishora (qo‘l harakati)', 'жест', 'noun', 'He used a friendly gesture.', 'U do‘stona ishora qildi.', 'ˈdʒestʃə'], ['posture', 'qomat', 'осанка', 'noun', 'Good posture shows confidence.', 'To‘g‘ri qomat ishonchni ko‘rsatadi.', 'ˈpɒstʃə'], ['proxemics', 'masofa tushunchasi', 'проксемика', 'noun', 'Proxemics studies personal space.', 'Proksemika shaxsiy masofani o‘rganadi.', 'prɒkˈsiːmɪks'], ['paralanguage', 'paralingvistika', 'паралингвистика', 'noun', 'Paralanguage includes tone and pace.', 'Paralingvistika ohang va tezlikni o‘z ichiga oladi.', 'ˌpærəˈlæŋɡwɪdʒ']],
      g: [['I was ___ if you’d mind opening the window.', 'wondering', 'wonder', 'wondered', 'Muloyim so‘rov.'], ['“It’s cold in here” may ___ a request.', 'be', 'being', 'to be', 'may + asosiy fe’l.']],
      dlg: ['Teacher: A guest says “It’s a bit warm in here.” What does this mean? Student: Literally, it describes the temperature. Teacher: And pragmatically? Student: It could be a request to open a window. Teacher: Yes, that’s implicature. Eye contact and gestures also carry meaning.', 'What is the pragmatic meaning?', 'A request to open a window', 'A weather report', 'A complaint about price', 'The student says “a request to open a window”.'],
      rd: ['Communication involves much more than words. Researchers estimate that a large share of the emotional message is conveyed through tone of voice, facial expression and posture. Yet body language is not universal: while maintaining eye contact signals honesty in some cultures, it may seem confrontational in others. Pragmatic competence also requires understanding implied meaning. When someone says “Could you possibly lend me a hand?”, the polite form conceals a request that is difficult to refuse. Learners often struggle with these subtleties even when their grammar is accurate.', ['Why do learners struggle with pragmatics?', 'Subtle implied meaning, even with accurate grammar', 'They lack vocabulary only', 'They speak too fast'], ['Body language is universal.', 'False'], ['Researchers studied 300 speakers.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Explain with three examples how the same sentence can carry different pragmatic meanings (150 words).', 'implicature|request|literally|context', 110, 'The sentence “It’s cold in here” is literally a statement about temperature. In context, it may be a request to close the window. “Could you possibly help me?” is grammatically a question, but pragmatically a polite request. Similarly, “That’s interesting” may express real interest or polite disagreement. Context and tone determine meaning.'],
      sp: ['Give an example of an indirect request in English and explain it.', 'I was wondering if you could help me. It is an indirect request, more polite than a direct command.'],
      ts: [['Hidden meaning of an utterance:', 'implicature', 'posture', 'gesture'], ['“Qomat” in English:', 'posture', 'proxemics', 'gesture'], ['I was ___ if you could help.', 'wondering', 'wonder', 'to wonder']] },
    { t: ['Idioms and expressions', 'Idiomalar va iboralar'],
      obj: ['Ingliz idiomalarini ma’nosi, uslubi va qo‘llanilishi bilan o‘zlashtirasiz.', 'You will master English idioms with their meaning, register and use.'],
      ex: ['Idiomalar o‘zgarmas ibora, ma’nosi so‘zlarning yig‘indisidan chiqmaydi: a piece of cake (juda oson), break the ice (muloqotni boshlash), hit the nail on the head (aniq topmoq), under the weather (o‘zini yomon his qilmoq), the last straw (sabr kosasini to‘ldirgan narsa), in the same boat (bir ahvolda), bite the bullet (qiyinchilikka bardosh bermoq).\nQoidalar: idiomaning shaklini o‘zgartirmang; uslubga e’tibor (ko‘pchilik norasmiy); haddan ortiq ishlatmang. Maqsad: ta’kidlash va tasvirlash. Kategoriya: weather idioms, body idioms, money idioms.',  'Idioms are fixed expressions whose meaning is not the sum of their words: a piece of cake (very easy), break the ice (start conversation), hit the nail on the head (be exactly right), under the weather (feeling ill), the last straw (the final thing that makes you lose patience), in the same boat (in the same situation), bite the bullet (face something unpleasant).\nRules: do not change the form; watch the register (most are informal); do not overuse. Purpose: emphasis and imagery. Categories: weather idioms, body idioms, money idioms.'],
      eg: [['The exam was a piece of cake.', 'Imtihon juda oson edi.'], ['He told a joke to break the ice.', 'U muzni buzish uchun hazil aytdi.'], ['You hit the nail on the head.', 'Siz aynan to‘g‘ri topdingiz.'], ['We’re all in the same boat.', 'Hammamiz bir ahvoldamiz.']],
      w: [['idiom', 'idioma', 'идиома', 'noun', 'An idiom has a fixed form.', 'Idiomaning shakli o‘zgarmas.', 'ˈɪdiəm'], ['figurative', 'ko‘chma ma’noli', 'переносный', 'adjective', 'Idioms have figurative meaning.', 'Idiomalar ko‘chma ma’noga ega.', 'ˈfɪɡjərətɪv'], ['literal', 'to‘g‘ri ma’noli', 'буквальный', 'adjective', 'The literal meaning is different.', 'To‘g‘ri ma’no boshqacha.', 'ˈlɪtərəl'], ['the last straw', 'sabr kosasini to‘ldirgan narsa', 'последняя капля', 'phrase', 'His rudeness was the last straw.', 'Uning qo‘pol gapi sabr kosasini to‘ldirdi.', 'ðə lɑːst strɔː'], ['bite the bullet', 'qiyinchilikka bardosh bermoq', 'стиснуть зубы', 'phrase', 'I had to bite the bullet and apologise.', 'Chidab, uzr so‘rashga to‘g‘ri keldi.', 'baɪt ðə ˈbʊlɪt'], ['break the ice', 'muloqotni boshlamoq', 'растопить лёд', 'phrase', 'A joke can break the ice.', 'Hazil muzni buzishi mumkin.', 'breɪk ðɪ aɪs']],
      g: [['He told a joke to ___ the ice.', 'break', 'broke', 'breaking', 'to + asosiy fe’l.'], ['We’re all in the same ___.', 'boat', 'ship', 'car', 'Idioma: in the same boat.']],
      dlg: ['Ali: How was the exam? Dilnoza: A piece of cake! Ali: I was under the weather and couldn’t revise. Dilnoza: We’re all in the same boat sometimes. You just have to bite the bullet and try again. Ali: You hit the nail on the head.', 'What does “a piece of cake” mean?', 'Very easy', 'Delicious', 'Expensive', 'It means the exam was very easy.'],
      rd: ['Idioms add colour to language, yet they are a notorious challenge for learners because their meaning cannot be guessed from the individual words. “It’s raining cats and dogs” has nothing to do with animals. Native speakers use idioms constantly, particularly in informal speech and journalism, but they are less common in formal academic writing, where they may sound too casual. The best way to learn idioms is in context, noting the situation in which they are used and the attitude they convey.', ['Where are idioms less common?', 'In formal academic writing', 'In informal speech', 'In journalism'], ['Idioms are easy to guess.', 'False'], ['“Raining cats and dogs” originated in the 16th century.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Write a short story (150 words) containing at least five idioms from this lesson, and underline them.', 'piece of cake|break the ice|in the same boat|bite the bullet', 110, 'On my first day at university I was nervous, so I told a joke to break the ice. It worked; everyone was in the same boat. The first lecture was a piece of cake, but the second was harder. I was under the weather, yet I decided to bite the bullet and finish my work. When my friend said I would pass, I felt she had hit the nail on the head.'],
      sp: ['Explain an idiom to a friend with an example.', '“Break the ice” means to start a conversation. For example, I told a joke to break the ice.'],
      ts: [['Meaning of “the last straw”:', 'the final thing that makes you lose patience', 'a drink', 'a little help'], ['“Idioma” in English:', 'idiom', 'figure', 'literal'], ['The test was a piece of ___.', 'cake', 'cakes', 'cook']] },
    { t: ['Collocations', 'Kollokatsiyalar'],
      obj: ['Kuchli kollokatsiyalar bilan tabiiy va aniq yozasiz va gapirasiz.', 'You will write and speak naturally and precisely with strong collocations.'],
      ex: ['Kollokatsiya — birga keladigan so‘zlar: make a decision (✗ do a decision), take a risk, heavy traffic, bitterly cold, deeply concerned, strongly recommend, fierce competition, widespread belief. Turlari: verb + noun, adjective + noun, adverb + adjective, noun + noun (a sense of humour). Sabab: tabiiylik va aniqlik. Xato: strong rain ✗ → heavy rain ✓.\nO‘rganish: bloklar bilan yodlang. Akademik kollokatsiyalar: raise concerns, gain insight, pose a threat, face a challenge, yield results, bear in mind.', 'A collocation is a combination of words that naturally go together: make a decision (✗ do a decision), take a risk, heavy traffic, bitterly cold, deeply concerned, strongly recommend, fierce competition, widespread belief. Types: verb + noun, adjective + noun, adverb + adjective, noun + noun (a sense of humour). Purpose: naturalness and precision. Error: strong rain ✗ → heavy rain ✓.\nLearning: memorise in chunks. Academic collocations: raise concerns, gain insight, pose a threat, face a challenge, yield results, bear in mind.'],
      eg: [['She made a difficult decision.', 'U qiyin qaror qabul qildi.'], ['We are deeply concerned about the results.', 'Natijalardan jiddiy xavotirdamiz.'], ['The study yielded interesting results.', 'Tadqiqot qiziqarli natijalar berdi.'], ['Bear in mind that costs may rise.', 'Xarajatlar oshishi mumkinligini yodda tuting.']],
      w: [['collocation', 'kollokatsiya', 'словосочетание', 'noun', 'Learn collocations, not single words.', 'Alohida so‘zlarni emas, kollokatsiyalarni o‘rganing.', 'ˌkɒləˈkeɪʃn'], ['fierce', 'qattiq (raqobat)', 'жёсткий', 'adjective', 'The competition is fierce.', 'Raqobat qattiq.', 'fɪəs'], ['widespread', 'keng tarqalgan', 'широко распространённый', 'adjective', 'It is a widespread belief.', 'Bu keng tarqalgan e’tiqod.', 'ˈwaɪdspred'], ['yield', 'natija bermoq', 'приносить результат', 'verb', 'The experiment yielded results.', 'Tajriba natija berdi.', 'jiːld'], ['pose', 'tahdid solmoq', 'представлять (угрозу)', 'verb', 'It poses a threat.', 'Bu tahdid solmoqda.', 'pəʊz'], ['bitterly', 'qattiq (sovuq/afsus)', 'мучительно', 'adverb', 'It was bitterly cold.', 'Havo qattiq sovuq edi.', 'ˈbɪtəli']],
      g: [['She ___ a difficult decision.', 'made', 'did', 'took of', 'make a decision.'], ['It was ___ cold that night.', 'bitterly', 'strongly', 'heavily', 'bitterly cold.']],
      dlg: ['Tutor: Which is correct: “do a decision” or “make a decision”? Student: Make a decision. Tutor: And “strong rain”? Student: Heavy rain. Tutor: Good. Learn collocations in chunks: raise concerns, face a challenge, yield results.', 'Which verb goes with “decision”?', 'Make', 'Do', 'Give', 'The student says “Make a decision”.'],
      rd: ['Native-like fluency depends heavily on collocation. Learners who know the word “strong” but not that we say “heavy rain” and “strong coffee” often sound slightly unnatural, even if their grammar is correct. Corpus studies show that a large proportion of everyday language is made up of fixed or semi-fixed chunks. Teachers therefore recommend noting new vocabulary together with its typical partners, and using collocation dictionaries when writing.', ['What do teachers recommend?', 'Noting words together with typical partners', 'Learning single words only', 'Avoiding dictionaries'], ['“Strong rain” is the natural collocation.', 'False'], ['Corpus studies began in 1950.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Write a paragraph (150 words) on a social issue using at least eight academic collocations from this lesson.', 'raise concerns|face a challenge|pose a threat|bear in mind', 110, 'Unemployment poses a serious threat to young people. Many graduates face a challenge when seeking their first job. Experts raise concerns about the skills gap. Employers, in turn, report fierce competition for experienced workers. It is worth bearing in mind that new initiatives may yield results only in the long term.'],
      sp: ['Say three sentences using strong collocations.', 'The company faces fierce competition. It must make a decision soon. The results will bear out the strategy.'],
      ts: [['Natural collocation:', 'heavy traffic', 'strong traffic', 'big traffic'], ['“Natija bermoq” in English:', 'yield', 'pose', 'bear'], ['The research ___ useful results.', 'yielded', 'made out', 'did']] },
    { t: ['Advanced phrasal verbs', 'Murakkab frazali fe’llar'],
      obj: ['Murakkab frazali fe’llarni aniq ma’no va uslub bilan ishlatasiz.', 'You will use advanced phrasal verbs with precise meaning and register.'],
      ex: ['Frazali fe’llar turlari: separable (look up a word / look a word up), inseparable (look after children), three-part (look forward to, put up with, come up with). Ma’no: ko‘pincha ko‘p ma’noli (take off: ko‘tarilmoq / yechmoq / mashhur bo‘lmoq). Uslub: norasmiy; rasmiyda sinonim: put off → postpone; carry out → conduct; find out → discover; bring about → cause.\nMisollar: come across (tasodifan uchratmoq), fall through (barbod bo‘lmoq), live up to (… ni oqlamoq), get away with, back up, phase out, rule out, play down. Qoida: olmosh ajraladigan fe’lning o‘rtasida: look it up (✗ look up it).', 'Types: separable (look up a word / look a word up), inseparable (look after children), three-part (look forward to, put up with, come up with). Meaning: often multi-meaning (take off: leave the ground / remove / become successful). Register: informal; formal synonyms: put off → postpone; carry out → conduct; find out → discover; bring about → cause.\nExamples: come across, fall through, live up to, get away with, back up, phase out, rule out, play down. Rule: a pronoun goes between separable parts: look it up (✗ look up it).'],
      eg: [['The deal fell through at the last minute.', 'Kelishuv oxirgi daqiqada barbod bo‘ldi.'], ['The film didn’t live up to expectations.', 'Film kutganlarni oqlamadi.'], ['They decided to phase out plastic bags.', 'Ular plastik paketlardan bosqichma-bosqich voz kechishga qaror qilishdi.'], ['We can’t rule out the possibility.', 'Bu ehtimolni inkor qila olmaymiz.']],
      w: [['fall through', 'barbod bo‘lmoq', 'сорваться', 'phrasal verb', 'The plan fell through.', 'Reja barbod bo‘ldi.', 'fɔːl θruː'], ['live up to', 'oqlamoq (kutilganni)', 'оправдывать', 'phrasal verb', 'It lived up to its reputation.', 'U obro‘sini oqladi.', 'lɪv ʌp tuː'], ['phase out', 'bosqichma-bosqich to‘xtatmoq', 'постепенно прекращать', 'phrasal verb', 'They phased out old models.', 'Ular eski modellarni bosqichma-bosqich to‘xtatdi.', 'feɪz aʊt'], ['rule out', 'inkor qilmoq', 'исключать', 'phrasal verb', 'Doctors ruled out infection.', 'Shifokorlar infektsiyani inkor qildi.', 'ruːl aʊt'], ['play down', 'ahamiyatsiz qilib ko‘rsatmoq', 'преуменьшать', 'phrasal verb', 'He played down the risk.', 'U xavfni ahamiyatsiz qilib ko‘rsatdi.', 'pleɪ daʊn'], ['come across', 'tasodifan uchratmoq', 'наткнуться', 'phrasal verb', 'I came across an old photo.', 'Men tasodifan eski suratga duch keldim.', 'kʌm əˈkrɒs']],
      g: [['The film didn’t ___ up to expectations.', 'live', 'lived', 'living', 'didn’t + asosiy fe’l.'], ['I can’t find the word, so I’ll look ___ up.', 'it', 'up it', 'to it', 'Olmosh o‘rtada.']],
      dlg: ['Manager: The merger fell through. Analyst: Why? Manager: The board couldn’t rule out financial risks. Analyst: Did the company play down the problems? Manager: Yes, and the new product didn’t live up to expectations, so they phased it out.', 'What happened to the merger?', 'It fell through', 'It succeeded', 'It was phased out', 'The manager says “The merger fell through”.'],
      rd: ['Phrasal verbs are among the most flexible features of English, and advanced learners are expected to use them accurately. Many have formal single-word equivalents, but the phrasal form often sounds more natural in speech: “find out” rather than “ascertain”. However, phrasal verbs can also be polysemous: “take off” may describe a plane leaving the ground, removing clothing or a business becoming successful. Context is therefore essential. Learners should record phrasal verbs in sentences rather than in isolated lists.', ['How should learners record phrasal verbs?', 'In sentences', 'In isolated lists', 'Only in speech'], ['Phrasal verbs have only one meaning.', 'False'], ['Phrasal verbs are used only in speech.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Write a short news report (150 words) using at least six advanced phrasal verbs.', 'fell through|ruled out|phased out|live up to', 110, 'The merger between the two companies fell through yesterday. Executives could not rule out financial risks, and the board decided to phase out several products. A spokesperson tried to play down the problems, but analysts say the company failed to live up to expectations. A new plan will be drawn up next month.'],
      sp: ['Tell about a plan that fell through, using two phrasal verbs.', 'Our trip fell through because we couldn’t rule out bad weather. We had to put it off.'],
      ts: [['Plan failed:', 'fell through', 'phased out', 'ruled out'], ['“Inkor qilmoq” (phrasal):', 'rule out', 'play down', 'look up'], ['Look it ___.', 'up', 'on', 'for it']] },
  ], exam: [['The deal ___ through at the last minute.', 'fell', 'fall', 'falls out', 'grammar'], ['Natural collocation:', 'make a decision', 'do a decision', 'create a decision', 'vocabulary'], ['“Idioma” in English:', 'idiom', 'figurative', 'proverb', 'vocabulary']] },

  { title: 'Science and ethics', lessons: [
    { t: ['The scientific method', 'Ilmiy usul'],
      obj: ['Ilmiy usul bosqichlarini va ilmiy fikrlash tilini aniq qo‘llaysiz.', 'You will accurately use the stages of the scientific method and the language of scientific reasoning.'],
      ex: ['Bosqichlar: observation → question → hypothesis (farazi) → experiment → analysis → conclusion → replication (takrorlash). Atamalar: theory, law, falsifiable (rad etilishi mumkin), peer review, bias, control, causation vs correlation. Til: The hypothesis was supported / rejected. The data are consistent with…, The experiment failed to replicate…\nMajhul va nominalizatsiya: The samples were heated to 100 degrees. Passiv + modal: The results should be interpreted with caution. Fikr: A hypothesis is testable; a theory is well substantiated.',  'Stages: observation → question → hypothesis → experiment → analysis → conclusion → replication. Terms: theory, law, falsifiable, peer review, bias, control, causation vs correlation. Language: The hypothesis was supported / rejected. The data are consistent with…, The experiment failed to replicate…\nPassive and nominalisation: The samples were heated to 100 degrees. Modal passive: The results should be interpreted with caution. A hypothesis is testable; a theory is well substantiated.'],
      eg: [['The hypothesis was supported by the data.', 'Faraz ma’lumotlar bilan qo‘llab-quvvatlandi.'], ['The results should be interpreted with caution.', 'Natijalarni ehtiyotkorlik bilan sharhlash kerak.'], ['Correlation does not imply causation.', 'Korrelyatsiya sababiyatni anglatmaydi.'], ['The experiment failed to replicate the original findings.', 'Tajriba asl topilmalarni takrorlay olmadi.']],
      w: [['hypothesis', 'faraz', 'гипотеза', 'noun', 'Test the hypothesis.', 'Farazni sinab ko‘ring.', 'haɪˈpɒθəsɪs'], ['falsifiable', 'rad etilishi mumkin bo‘lgan', 'опровержимый', 'adjective', 'A good hypothesis is falsifiable.', 'Yaxshi faraz rad etilishi mumkin bo‘ladi.', 'ˈfɔːlsɪfaɪəbl'], ['replicate', 'takrorlamoq (tajribani)', 'воспроизводить', 'verb', 'Other labs tried to replicate it.', 'Boshqa laboratoriyalar uni takrorlashga urindi.', 'ˈreplɪkeɪt'], ['causation', 'sababiyat', 'причинность', 'noun', 'Causation is hard to prove.', 'Sababiyatni isbotlash qiyin.', 'kɔːˈzeɪʃn'], ['correlation', 'korrelyatsiya', 'корреляция', 'noun', 'There is a correlation between the two.', 'Ikkalasi o‘rtasida korrelyatsiya bor.', 'ˌkɒrəˈleɪʃn'], ['substantiate', 'asoslamoq (dalil bilan)', 'обосновывать', 'verb', 'The claim was not substantiated.', 'Da’vo dalil bilan asoslanmadi.', 'səbˈstænʃieɪt']],
      g: [['The hypothesis ___ supported by the data.', 'was', 'were', 'has be', 'Majhul o‘tgan.'], ['The results should ___ interpreted with caution.', 'be', 'been', 'being', 'should be + V3.']],
      dlg: ['Professor: Your hypothesis must be falsifiable. Student: Meaning that it can be proven wrong? Professor: Yes. Otherwise it is not scientific. Student: Our results show a correlation. Professor: Be careful: correlation does not imply causation, and the experiment should be replicated.', 'What does the professor warn about?', 'Correlation does not imply causation', 'The cost', 'The lab size', 'The professor says so.'],
      rd: ['The scientific method is not a rigid recipe but a systematic way of testing ideas. A researcher starts with an observation, formulates a hypothesis and designs an experiment that could prove it wrong. The results are analysed, shared through peer-reviewed journals and, crucially, replicated by independent teams. Science advances not because theories are proven true, but because they survive repeated attempts to disprove them. A theory that cannot, even in principle, be falsified is generally regarded as outside the scope of science.', ['Why are results replicated?', 'To check whether they survive independent tests', 'To make the research longer', 'To publish more articles'], ['A good hypothesis cannot be disproved.', 'False'], ['Peer review began in 1665.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Describe a simple experiment (150 words) that tests a hypothesis, using passive forms and scientific language.', 'the hypothesis|were|the results|should be interpreted', 110, 'The hypothesis was that students who sleep eight hours perform better. Fifty participants were divided into two groups. One group was asked to sleep eight hours, while the other slept six. Test scores were compared after a week. The results indicated a significant difference, but they should be interpreted with caution. The experiment should be replicated with a larger sample.'],
      sp: ['Explain the difference between correlation and causation.', 'Correlation means two things occur together. Causation means one causes the other. Correlation does not imply causation.'],
      ts: [['A testable proposed explanation:', 'hypothesis', 'theory', 'law'], ['“Takrorlamoq” (tajribani):', 'replicate', 'substantiate', 'cause'], ['The data ___ consistent with the theory.', 'are', 'is', 'was be']] },
    { t: ['Bioethics', 'Bioetika'],
      obj: ['Tibbiyot va biotexnologiyadagi axloqiy muammolarni muhokama qilasiz.', 'You will discuss ethical issues in medicine and biotechnology.'],
      ex: ['Bioetika: informed consent (ma’lumotli rozilik), genetic engineering (genetik muhandislik), gene editing (CRISPR), cloning, stem cells, euthanasia, organ donation, clinical trial, confidentiality, autonomy vs beneficence. Axloq tamoyillari: autonomy (mustaqillik), beneficence (yaxshilik qilish), non-maleficence (zarar yetkazmaslik), justice.\nTuzilmalar: Whether it is ethical to… remains a matter of debate. Proponents argue that…, whereas opponents contend that… Gipotetik: Were gene editing to become routine, society would have to decide…',  'Bioethics: informed consent, genetic engineering, gene editing (CRISPR), cloning, stem cells, euthanasia, organ donation, clinical trial, confidentiality, autonomy vs beneficence. Principles: autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, justice.\nStructures: Whether it is ethical to… remains a matter of debate. Proponents argue that…, whereas opponents contend that… Hypothetical: Were gene editing to become routine, society would have to decide…'],
      eg: [['Patients must give informed consent before treatment.', 'Bemorlar davolanishdan oldin ma’lumotli rozilik berishi kerak.'], ['Proponents argue that gene editing could cure diseases.', 'Tarafdorlar gen tahriri kasalliklarni davolashi mumkin deydi.'], ['Opponents contend that it may lead to inequality.', 'Raqiblar bu tengsizlikka olib kelishi mumkin deb da’vo qiladi.'], ['Were gene editing to become routine, society would face new choices.', 'Gen tahriri odatiy bo‘lsa, jamiyat yangi tanlovlarga duch kelardi.']],
      w: [['informed consent', 'ma’lumotli rozilik', 'информированное согласие', 'noun', 'Informed consent is mandatory.', 'Ma’lumotli rozilik majburiy.', 'ɪnˈfɔːmd kənˈsent'], ['autonomy', 'mustaqillik (tanlov huquqi)', 'автономия', 'noun', 'Respect patient autonomy.', 'Bemor mustaqilligini hurmat qiling.', 'ɔːˈtɒnəmi'], ['clinical trial', 'klinik sinov', 'клиническое испытание', 'noun', 'The drug is in a clinical trial.', 'Dori klinik sinovda.', 'ˈklɪnɪkl ˈtraɪəl'], ['proponent', 'tarafdor', 'сторонник', 'noun', 'Proponents support the law.', 'Tarafdorlar qonunni qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi.', 'prəˈpəʊnənt'], ['contend', 'da’vo qilmoq (munozarada)', 'утверждать', 'verb', 'Critics contend that it is unsafe.', 'Tanqidchilar uni xavfli deb da’vo qiladi.', 'kənˈtend'], ['beneficence', 'yaxshilik qilish tamoyili', 'благодеяние', 'noun', 'Beneficence guides doctors.', 'Yaxshilik tamoyili shifokorlarga yo‘l ko‘rsatadi.', 'bɪˈnefɪsns']],
      g: [['Patients must give informed ___.', 'consent', 'consensus', 'content', 'Informed consent.'], ['___ gene editing to become routine, society would face new choices.', 'Were', 'Was', 'Had', 'Inversiya.']],
      dlg: ['Moderator: Should parents be allowed to edit their children’s genes? Proponent: It could prevent serious diseases. Opponent: Opponents contend that it may lead to inequality. Proponent: Whether it is ethical remains a matter of debate. Moderator: Informed consent is also problematic, since the child cannot give it.', 'Why is consent problematic?', 'The child cannot give it', 'Parents refuse', 'Doctors disagree', 'The moderator says the child cannot give it.'],
      rd: ['Advances in genetic engineering have opened remarkable possibilities, from curing inherited diseases to producing drought-resistant crops. Yet they also raise ethical questions. Proponents argue that preventing suffering is a moral duty, whereas opponents contend that altering human genes risks creating inequality between those who can afford enhancements and those who cannot. Bioethicists generally appeal to four principles: respect for autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence and justice. Where these principles conflict, careful public debate is essential.', ['What do bioethicists appeal to?', 'Autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence and justice', 'Profit and speed', 'Popular opinion only'], ['Opponents argue that gene editing guarantees equality.', 'False'], ['CRISPR was discovered in Japan.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Write a balanced paragraph (180 words) on whether gene editing in humans should be permitted; include proponents and opponents.', 'proponents argue|opponents contend|were|remains a matter of debate', 120, 'Whether gene editing in humans should be permitted remains a matter of debate. Proponents argue that it could cure serious inherited diseases and reduce suffering. Opponents contend that it may lead to inequality and unintended consequences. Were the technology to become widely available, regulation would be essential. On balance, therapeutic uses may be justified, but enhancement remains ethically controversial.'],
      sp: ['Give your view on organ donation policy in one minute, covering benefits and concerns.', 'Organ donation saves lives. However, autonomy and consent must be respected. I believe a clear opt-in system is the fairest approach.'],
      ts: [['The right to make one’s own medical decisions:', 'autonomy', 'beneficence', 'justice'], ['“Tarafdor” in English:', 'proponent', 'opponent', 'consent'], ['Opponents ___ that it is unsafe.', 'contend', 'contends', 'contending to']] },
    { t: ['Technology and responsibility', 'Texnologiya va mas’uliyat'],
      obj: ['Texnologiya yaratuvchilari va foydalanuvchilarining mas’uliyatini tahlil qilasiz.', 'You will analyse the responsibility of technology creators and users.'],
      ex: ['Mas’uliyat: accountability (javobgarlik), liability (huquqiy javobgarlik), regulation, transparency, unintended consequences (kutilmagan oqibatlar), dual-use technology (ikki maqsadli), precautionary principle (ehtiyotkorlik tamoyili). Fe’l shakllari: be held accountable, bear responsibility, be liable for.\nAraliq shartli va tavsiya: Should the technology prove harmful, developers ought to be held accountable. (Inversiya.) Siyosat: It is incumbent on governments to… (hukumatning burchi). Mantiq: By the same token,… (xuddi shu sababli); That being said,… (shunga qaramay).', 'Responsibility: accountability, liability, regulation, transparency, unintended consequences, dual-use technology, precautionary principle. Verb patterns: be held accountable, bear responsibility, be liable for.\nConditionals and obligation: Should the technology prove harmful, developers ought to be held accountable (inversion). Policy: It is incumbent on governments to… Logic: By the same token,… ; That being said,…'],
      eg: [['Should the technology prove harmful, developers ought to be held accountable.', 'Texnologiya zararli bo‘lsa, ishlab chiquvchilar javobgar bo‘lishi kerak.'], ['It is incumbent on governments to regulate it.', 'Uni tartibga solish hukumatlarning burchi.'], ['By the same token, users also bear responsibility.', 'Xuddi shu sababli foydalanuvchilar ham mas’uliyat ko‘taradi.'], ['That being said, innovation should not be stifled.', 'Shunga qaramay, innovatsiyani bo‘g‘ib qo‘ymaslik kerak.']],
      w: [['accountability', 'javobgarlik (hisobdorlik)', 'подотчётность', 'noun', 'Accountability builds trust.', 'Hisobdorlik ishonchni oshiradi.', 'əˌkaʊntəˈbɪləti'], ['liable', 'huquqiy javobgar', 'ответственный по закону', 'adjective', 'The company is liable for damages.', 'Kompaniya zarar uchun javobgar.', 'ˈlaɪəbl'], ['transparency', 'shaffoflik', 'прозрачность', 'noun', 'Transparency is essential for AI.', 'Sun’iy intellekt uchun shaffoflik zarur.', 'trænsˈpærənsi'], ['dual-use', 'ikki maqsadli', 'двойного назначения', 'adjective', 'Drones are dual-use technology.', 'Dronlar ikki maqsadli texnologiya.', 'ˈdjuːəl juːs'], ['incumbent', 'burch (mas’uliyat)', 'обязанный', 'adjective', 'It is incumbent on you to report it.', 'Bu haqda xabar berish sizning burchingiz.', 'ɪnˈkʌmbənt'], ['stifle', 'bo‘g‘ib qo‘ymoq', 'подавлять', 'verb', 'Over-regulation may stifle innovation.', 'Haddan ortiq tartibga solish innovatsiyani bo‘g‘ishi mumkin.', 'ˈstaɪfl']],
      g: [['___ the technology prove harmful, developers ought to be held accountable.', 'Should', 'Would', 'Did', 'Inversiya: Should + subyekt + fe’l.'], ['It is ___ on governments to regulate it.', 'incumbent', 'incumbency', 'incumber', 'Burch — incumbent.']],
      dlg: ['Reporter: Who is responsible when an algorithm discriminates? Lawyer: Developers should be held accountable, and companies are liable for damages. Reporter: What about transparency? Lawyer: It is incumbent on regulators to demand it. That being said, we must not stifle innovation.', 'What does the lawyer say about innovation?', 'It should not be stifled', 'It should be banned', 'It is unimportant', 'She says “we must not stifle innovation”.'],
      rd: ['Every powerful technology can serve good or harmful purposes. Drones deliver medicine but may also be used for surveillance; facial recognition can find missing children but also track citizens. Because of such dual-use potential, scholars argue for the precautionary principle: when the consequences are uncertain but potentially severe, developers should act cautiously. At the same time, over-regulation could stifle innovation. The challenge is therefore to design systems of accountability that are strict enough to protect people yet flexible enough to allow progress.', ['What does the precautionary principle recommend?', 'Acting cautiously when consequences are uncertain but severe', 'Ignoring risks', 'Banning all innovation'], ['Technology always serves harmful purposes.', 'False'], ['Drones were invented for medicine.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Write an essay paragraph (180 words): “Developers, not users, should be held responsible for the misuse of technology.”', 'should be held accountable|by the same token|that being said|incumbent', 120, 'It is often argued that developers should be held accountable for the misuse of technology. By the same token, users bear responsibility for their choices. That being said, creators are best placed to foresee risks. It is incumbent on governments to require transparency. Should a product prove harmful, the company ought to be liable. Responsibility should therefore be shared.'],
      sp: ['Say who should be responsible when technology causes harm, in four sentences.', 'Developers should be held accountable. By the same token, users bear responsibility. That being said, regulators must ensure transparency.'],
      ts: [['Responsible by law:', 'liable', 'transparent', 'dual-use'], ['“Shaffoflik” in English:', 'transparency', 'accountability', 'liability'], ['It is ___ on us to act.', 'incumbent', 'incumbency', 'incumbently']] },
    { t: ['Reading science texts', 'Ilmiy matnlarni o‘qish'],
      obj: ['Murakkab ilmiy matnlarni o‘qib, asosiy g‘oya, dalil va cheklovlarni ajratib olasiz.', 'You will read complex scientific texts and extract the main idea, evidence and limitations.'],
      ex: ['Ilmiy matn tuzilmasi: Abstract → Introduction → Methods → Results → Discussion. Strategiya: avval abstract va conclusion, keyin methods. Tilni tushunish: nominal ibora (the gradual acidification of oceans = okeanlarning asta-sekin kislotalanishi), uzun gaplar, majhul, hedging. Savollar: main idea, supporting detail, inference, author’s attitude, vocabulary in context.\nKo‘rsatkich so‘zlar: the vast majority, a negligible (ahamiyatsiz) proportion, markedly, substantially, merely. Ehtiyotkorlik: suggest, indicate, appear. Notanish so‘z: prefikslar (hypo-, anti-, micro-), ildizlar, kontekst.', 'Structure of scientific articles: Abstract → Introduction → Methods → Results → Discussion. Strategy: read the abstract and conclusion first, then methods. Language: noun phrases (the gradual acidification of oceans), long sentences, passive, hedging. Questions: main idea, supporting detail, inference, author’s attitude, vocabulary in context.\nQuantity words: the vast majority, a negligible proportion, markedly, substantially, merely. Caution: suggest, indicate, appear. Unknown words: prefixes (hypo-, anti-, micro-), roots, context.'],
      eg: [['The gradual acidification of the oceans threatens coral reefs.', 'Okeanlarning asta-sekin kislotalanishi marjon riflariga tahdid soladi.'], ['A negligible proportion of the sample was affected.', 'Tanlanmaning ahamiyatsiz ulushiga ta’sir qildi.'], ['The data appear to indicate a downward trend.', 'Ma’lumotlar pasayish tendensiyasini ko‘rsatayotganga o‘xshaydi.'], ['Read the abstract first, then the conclusion.', 'Avval annotatsiyani, keyin xulosani o‘qing.']],
      w: [['abstract', 'annotatsiya', 'аннотация', 'noun', 'Read the abstract first.', 'Avval annotatsiyani o‘qing.', 'ˈæbstrækt'], ['acidification', 'kislotalanish', 'закисление', 'noun', 'Ocean acidification harms corals.', 'Okean kislotalanishi marjonlarga zarar.', 'əˌsɪdɪfɪˈkeɪʃn'], ['negligible', 'ahamiyatsiz', 'незначительный', 'adjective', 'The effect was negligible.', 'Ta’sir ahamiyatsiz edi.', 'ˈneɡlɪdʒəbl'], ['markedly', 'sezilarli darajada', 'заметно', 'adverb', 'Temperatures rose markedly.', 'Harorat sezilarli oshdi.', 'ˈmɑːkɪdli'], ['merely', 'faqat, xolos', 'всего лишь', 'adverb', 'It is merely a correlation.', 'Bu faqat korrelyatsiya.', 'ˈmɪəli'], ['substantially', 'sezilarli darajada', 'существенно', 'adverb', 'Costs fell substantially.', 'Xarajatlar sezilarli kamaydi.', 'səbˈstænʃəli']],
      g: [['Read the ___ first, then the conclusion.', 'abstract', 'abstraction', 'abstractly', 'Ot kerak.'], ['The data ___ to indicate a downward trend.', 'appear', 'appears', 'appearing', 'data — ko‘plik.']],
      dlg: ['Tutor: How do you read a scientific article quickly? Student: First the abstract, then the conclusion. Tutor: Then? Student: The methods, to check whether the study is reliable. Tutor: Watch for hedges like “appear” and “suggest”, and for words like “merely” that limit the claim.', 'What do you read first?', 'The abstract', 'The references', 'The appendix', 'The student says the abstract.'],
      rd: ['Abstract. Over the past forty years, the acidity of the world’s oceans has increased markedly as seawater absorbs carbon dioxide from the atmosphere. This study examined the effect of acidification on coral growth across fifty reef sites. The results suggest that coral calcification declined substantially at sites with the highest acidity, whereas the impact at low-acidity sites was negligible. However, the authors caution that laboratory conditions cannot fully replicate natural reefs, and that further field research is required.', ['What do the authors caution?', 'Laboratory conditions cannot fully replicate natural reefs', 'The study was too long', 'The reefs are growing'], ['Acidification has decreased over forty years.', 'False'], ['The study was funded by the government.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Write an abstract (120 words) for an imagined scientific study, with aim, method, main result and limitation.', 'this study|the results suggest|however|further research', 90, 'This study examined the relationship between sleep duration and memory in 200 students. Participants completed memory tests after sleeping for six or eight hours. The results suggest that longer sleep was associated with markedly better performance. However, the sample was limited to one university, and the effect may differ in other groups. Further research is required.'],
      sp: ['Summarise a scientific finding for a non-specialist in three sentences.', 'The study suggests that longer sleep improves memory. The effect was large. However, more research is needed.'],
      ts: [['A short summary at the start of an article:', 'abstract', 'appendix', 'index'], ['“Ahamiyatsiz” in English:', 'negligible', 'substantial', 'marked'], ['The effect was ___ small.', 'negligibly', 'negligible of', 'negligent']] },
  ], exam: [['___ the technology prove harmful, we must act.', 'Should', 'Did', 'Would', 'grammar'], ['The hypothesis was ___ by the data.', 'supported', 'supporting', 'support to', 'grammar'], ['“Huquqiy javobgar” in English:', 'liable', 'incumbent', 'transparent', 'vocabulary']] },

  { title: 'Politics and policy', lessons: [
    { t: ['Government and democracy', 'Hukumat va demokratiya'],
      obj: ['Hukumat tuzilmalari va demokratik jarayonlarni tushuntirasiz.', 'You will explain government structures and democratic processes.'],
      ex: ['Atamalar: constitution (konstitutsiya), parliament, executive / legislature / judiciary (ijroiya / qonun chiqaruvchi / sud), election, ballot (ovoz berish), turnout (faollik), coalition, majority, minority, referendum, checks and balances, separation of powers. Demokratiya turlari: direct vs representative; presidential vs parliamentary.\nFe’llar: elect, vote, abstain (betaraf qolmoq), amend (o‘zgartirmoq), enact (qabul qilmoq), repeal (bekor qilmoq). Majhul va rasmiy: The bill was passed by a narrow margin. Raqamlar: by a two-thirds majority, a turnout of 60 per cent.', 'Terms: constitution, parliament, executive / legislature / judiciary, election, ballot, turnout, coalition, majority, minority, referendum, checks and balances, separation of powers. Types: direct vs representative; presidential vs parliamentary.\nVerbs: elect, vote, abstain, amend, enact, repeal. Passive and formal: The bill was passed by a narrow margin. Figures: by a two-thirds majority, a turnout of 60 per cent.'],
      eg: [['The bill was passed by a narrow margin.', 'Qonun loyihasi kichik farq bilan qabul qilindi.'], ['Turnout was only 60 per cent.', 'Faollik atigi 60 foiz bo‘ldi.'], ['The constitution was amended in 2016.', 'Konstitutsiya 2016-yilda o‘zgartirildi.'], ['Three members abstained.', 'Uch a’zo betaraf qoldi.']],
      w: [['constitution', 'konstitutsiya', 'конституция', 'noun', 'The constitution protects rights.', 'Konstitutsiya huquqlarni himoya qiladi.', 'ˌkɒnstɪˈtjuːʃn'], ['legislature', 'qonun chiqaruvchi organ', 'законодательный орган', 'noun', 'The legislature passes laws.', 'Qonun chiqaruvchi organ qonun qabul qiladi.', 'ˈledʒɪsleɪtʃə'], ['turnout', 'saylovdagi faollik', 'явка', 'noun', 'Turnout was low.', 'Faollik past edi.', 'ˈtɜːnaʊt'], ['referendum', 'referendum', 'референдум', 'noun', 'A referendum was held.', 'Referendum o‘tkazildi.', 'ˌrefəˈrendəm'], ['abstain', 'betaraf qolmoq', 'воздерживаться', 'verb', 'Two members abstained.', 'Ikki a’zo betaraf qoldi.', 'æbˈsteɪn'], ['coalition', 'koalitsiya', 'коалиция', 'noun', 'A coalition governs the country.', 'Mamlakatni koalitsiya boshqaradi.', 'ˌkəʊəˈlɪʃn']],
      g: [['The bill ___ passed by a narrow margin.', 'was', 'were', 'is being', 'Majhul.'], ['Three members ___ from voting.', 'abstained', 'abstain', 'abstaining', 'O‘tgan zamon.']],
      dlg: ['Presenter: Turnout in the referendum was only 45 per cent. Analyst: That weakens the mandate. Presenter: The proposal was passed by a narrow margin. Analyst: Three members of the coalition abstained. Presenter: Will the constitution be amended? Analyst: Probably, though a two-thirds majority is required.', 'What majority is required to amend the constitution?', 'Two-thirds', 'One half', 'Unanimity', 'The analyst says “a two-thirds majority”.'],
      rd: ['In a representative democracy, citizens elect politicians to make decisions on their behalf. Power is usually divided among three branches: the legislature, which passes laws; the executive, which implements them; and the judiciary, which interprets them. This separation of powers, together with checks and balances, aims to prevent any one branch from becoming too powerful. Referendums allow citizens to decide specific questions directly, yet critics note that low turnout or complex questions can distort results.', ['What does the judiciary do?', 'Interprets laws', 'Passes laws', 'Implements laws'], ['In a representative democracy, citizens decide every issue.', 'False'], ['Referendums are held every year.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Explain how a law is made in your country or in a country you know (150 words).', 'the bill|passed|the executive|the legislature', 110, 'In most democracies a law begins as a bill. The legislature debates it, proposes amendments and votes. If a majority approves, the bill is passed. The executive then implements it, and the judiciary may interpret it in disputes. This separation of powers ensures checks and balances.'],
      sp: ['Explain the difference between direct and representative democracy.', 'In a direct democracy citizens vote on issues. In a representative democracy, they elect politicians to decide for them.'],
      ts: [['The branch that makes laws:', 'legislature', 'judiciary', 'executive'], ['“Betaraf qolmoq” in English:', 'abstain', 'amend', 'enact'], ['The constitution ___ amended last year.', 'was', 'were', 'has be']] },
    { t: ['A policy debate', 'Siyosat bo‘yicha munozara'],
      obj: ['Siyosat takliflarini tahlil qilib, ularni munozarada himoya qilasiz yoki rad etasiz.', 'You will analyse policy proposals and defend or challenge them in a debate.'],
      ex: ['Siyosat tahlili: cost–benefit analysis, feasibility (amalga oshirish imkoni), unintended consequences, stakeholders (manfaatdor tomonlar), implementation, funding, short-term vs long-term. Iboralar: The policy is feasible/unrealistic. Who stands to gain/lose? What are the trade-offs? (murosa), On the one hand… but at what cost?\nFikrni asoslash: This policy would disproportionately affect…, Evidence from other countries suggests… Savol: Is it a cost-effective measure? Natija: This could, in turn, give rise to…',  'Policy analysis: cost–benefit analysis, feasibility, unintended consequences, stakeholders, implementation, funding, short-term vs long-term. Phrases: The policy is feasible/unrealistic. Who stands to gain/lose? What are the trade-offs? On the one hand… but at what cost?\nSupporting claims: This policy would disproportionately affect…, Evidence from other countries suggests… Question: Is it a cost-effective measure? Result: This could, in turn, give rise to…'],
      eg: [['Who stands to gain from this policy?', 'Bu siyosatdan kim yutadi?'], ['What are the trade-offs?', 'Kelishuvlar (murosa) nimalardan iborat?'], ['This policy would disproportionately affect low-income families.', 'Bu siyosat past daromadli oilalarga nomutanosib ta’sir qiladi.'], ['It could, in turn, give rise to unintended consequences.', 'Bu, o‘z navbatida, kutilmagan oqibatlarga olib kelishi mumkin.']],
      w: [['feasible', 'amalga oshirsa bo‘ladigan', 'осуществимый', 'adjective', 'The plan is feasible.', 'Reja amalga oshirsa bo‘ladi.', 'ˈfiːzəbl'], ['stakeholder', 'manfaatdor tomon', 'заинтересованная сторона', 'noun', 'Consult all stakeholders.', 'Barcha manfaatdor tomonlar bilan maslahatlashing.', 'ˈsteɪkhəʊldə'], ['trade-off', 'murosa (bir narsani yutib, boshqasini yo‘qotish)', 'компромисс', 'noun', 'Every policy involves a trade-off.', 'Har bir siyosatda murosa bor.', 'ˈtreɪd ɒf'], ['disproportionately', 'nomutanosib ravishda', 'непропорционально', 'adverb', 'It disproportionately affects the poor.', 'U kambag‘allarga nomutanosib ta’sir qiladi.', 'ˌdɪsprəˈpɔːʃənətli'], ['cost-effective', 'tejamkor, samarali', 'экономически эффективный', 'adjective', 'It is a cost-effective measure.', 'Bu tejamkor chora.', 'ˌkɒst ɪˈfektɪv'], ['implementation', 'amalga oshirish', 'реализация', 'noun', 'Implementation will take years.', 'Amalga oshirish yillar oladi.', 'ˌɪmplɪmenˈteɪʃn']],
      g: [['Who ___ to gain from this policy?', 'stands', 'stand', 'standing', '“stands to gain”.'], ['The policy would ___ affect low-income families.', 'disproportionately', 'disproportion', 'disproportionate', 'Ravish kerak.']],
      dlg: ['Chair: The proposal is a congestion charge. Speaker A: It is feasible and cost-effective. Speaker B: But who stands to lose? It would disproportionately affect low-income commuters. Speaker A: Revenue could fund public transport. Speaker B: That is a trade-off, and implementation is uncertain.', 'What is Speaker B’s main concern?', 'It would disproportionately affect low-income commuters', 'The cost of the charge', 'Public transport', 'B says so.'],
      rd: ['Evaluating a policy requires more than asking whether it sounds good. Analysts consider its costs and benefits, who is likely to gain or lose, and whether it can actually be implemented. A congestion charge, for example, can reduce traffic and pollution, yet it may disproportionately affect low-income commuters unless revenue is invested in public transport. Experts also look at evidence from other cities, since a policy that works in one context may fail in another. The best proposals acknowledge trade-offs openly and include a plan for monitoring the results.', ['What do the best proposals include?', 'A plan for monitoring results', 'No evidence', 'No trade-offs'], ['Analysts only ask whether a policy sounds good.', 'False'], ['Congestion charges began in London.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Write a policy brief (250 words): recommend or reject a proposal (e.g. free public transport), covering benefits, costs, stakeholders and trade-offs.', 'who stands to|trade-off|feasible|in turn', 170, 'This brief evaluates the proposal to make public transport free. Its main benefit is accessibility: low-income families would stand to gain most. It could also reduce traffic. However, the policy is costly, and funding would have to come from taxation. This is a trade-off between access and public spending. If implemented gradually, the proposal appears feasible. It is recommended that a pilot scheme be launched in one city.'],
      sp: ['Argue for or against a policy in 60 seconds, mentioning one trade-off.', 'I support free public transport. It helps low-income families. The trade-off is cost, but pilot schemes can show if it is feasible.'],
      ts: [['A balance between two desirable things:', 'trade-off', 'stakeholder', 'turnout'], ['“Manfaatdor tomon” in English:', 'stakeholder', 'coalition', 'legislature'], ['The plan is ___.', 'feasible', 'feasibly', 'feasibility']] },
    { t: ['Law and justice', 'Qonun va adolat'],
      obj: ['Huquq tizimi, sud jarayoni va adolat tushunchalarini muhokama qilasiz.', 'You will discuss the legal system, court procedure and concepts of justice.'],
      ex: ['Huquqiy so‘zlar: court, judge, jury (hay’at), defendant (sudlanuvchi), prosecution, plaintiff (da’vogar), verdict (hukm), sentence (jazo), appeal (apellyatsiya), evidence, witness, lawyer / barrister, bail, innocent until proven guilty. Jinoyat / fuqarolik huquqi. Fe’llar: sue, convict (hukm qilmoq), acquit (oqlamoq), plead guilty, testify, uphold, overturn.\nMajhul: He was convicted of fraud and sentenced to five years. Aniqlovchi: a legal system which is based on… Mavhum tushunchalar: justice, fairness, equality before the law, the rule of law.', 'Legal words: court, judge, jury, defendant, prosecution, plaintiff, verdict, sentence, appeal, evidence, witness, lawyer / barrister, bail, innocent until proven guilty. Criminal / civil law. Verbs: sue, convict, acquit, plead guilty, testify, uphold, overturn.\nPassive: He was convicted of fraud and sentenced to five years. Relative: a legal system which is based on… Abstract: justice, fairness, equality before the law, the rule of law.'],
      eg: [['He was convicted of fraud and sentenced to five years.', 'U firibgarlikda aybdor deb topildi va besh yilga hukm qilindi.'], ['The jury acquitted the defendant.', 'Hay’at sudlanuvchini oqladi.'], ['She pleaded guilty to the lesser charge.', 'U yengilroq ayblovni tan oldi.'], ['The court of appeal overturned the verdict.', 'Apellyatsiya sudi hukmni bekor qildi.']],
      w: [['defendant', 'sudlanuvchi', 'подсудимый', 'noun', 'The defendant denied the charge.', 'Sudlanuvchi ayblovni rad etdi.', 'dɪˈfendənt'], ['verdict', 'hukm (hay’at)', 'вердикт', 'noun', 'The jury reached a verdict.', 'Hay’at hukmga keldi.', 'ˈvɜːdɪkt'], ['acquit', 'oqlamoq', 'оправдывать', 'verb', 'He was acquitted of all charges.', 'U barcha ayblovlardan oqlandi.', 'əˈkwɪt'], ['convict', 'aybdor deb topmoq', 'осуждать', 'verb', 'They convicted him of theft.', 'Ular uni o‘g‘rilikda aybdor deb topdi.', 'kənˈvɪkt'], ['testify', 'guvohlik bermoq', 'давать показания', 'verb', 'She testified in court.', 'U sudda guvohlik berdi.', 'ˈtestɪfaɪ'], ['uphold', 'kuchda qoldirmoq', 'поддерживать', 'verb', 'The court upheld the decision.', 'Sud qarorni kuchda qoldirdi.', 'ʌpˈhəʊld']],
      g: [['He was ___ of fraud and sentenced to five years.', 'convicted', 'convict', 'convicting', 'Majhul.'], ['The court of appeal ___ the verdict.', 'overturned', 'overturn', 'overturning', 'O‘tgan zamon.']],
      dlg: ['Reporter: What was the verdict? Lawyer: The jury acquitted the defendant. Reporter: Will the prosecution appeal? Lawyer: Possibly. The witness who testified changed her statement. Reporter: What principle applies? Lawyer: Innocent until proven guilty.', 'What principle does the lawyer mention?', 'Innocent until proven guilty', 'Guilty until proven innocent', 'Justice for the rich', 'She says so.'],
      rd: ['A fair legal system rests on several principles. Everyone is considered innocent until proven guilty, and the burden of proof lies with the prosecution. Defendants have the right to a lawyer and to a public trial, and evidence must be presented openly. If either side believes that the law was misapplied, they may appeal to a higher court, which can uphold or overturn the original decision. The rule of law, the idea that all people and institutions are subject to the same laws, is widely regarded as the foundation of justice.', ['Where does the burden of proof lie?', 'With the prosecution', 'With the defendant', 'With the jury'], ['Defendants have no right to a lawyer.', 'False'], ['The legal system was reformed in 1990.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Describe a fictional trial in 180 words: charges, evidence, verdict and consequences, using legal vocabulary and passive forms.', 'was convicted|acquitted|evidence|sentenced', 120, 'The defendant was charged with fraud. The prosecution presented documents and a witness testified about false invoices. The defence argued that the evidence was circumstantial. After two days, the jury reached a verdict: the defendant was convicted and sentenced to three years. His lawyer announced that she would appeal.'],
      sp: ['Explain the idea “innocent until proven guilty” in your own words.', 'It means the court must prove guilt. The defendant does not have to prove innocence.'],
      ts: [['A decision of the jury:', 'verdict', 'sentence', 'bail'], ['“Oqlamoq” in English:', 'acquit', 'convict', 'uphold'], ['The witness ___ in court yesterday.', 'testified', 'testify', 'testifies']] },
    { t: ['Politics in the media', 'Siyosat va OAV'],
      obj: ['Siyosiy xabarlar, bayonotlar va nutqlarni tanqidiy o‘qiysiz va tahlil qilasiz.', 'You will critically read and analyse political news, statements and speeches.'],
      ex: ['Siyosiy tili: spin doctor, soundbite (qisqa iqtibos), manifesto (dastur), campaign, propaganda, rhetoric (ritorika), partisan (partiyaviy), mandate (vakolat), poll (so‘rov), approval rating. Tanqidiy savollar: Who benefits? What is omitted? Is the language emotive? Are claims substantiated?\nFe’llar: pledge (va’da bermoq), vow, denounce (qoralamoq), endorse (qo‘llab-quvvatlamoq), dismiss. Neytral va kuchli fe’llar: say / state / claim / insist / allege. Davomli hisobot: The minister is reported to have said…',  'Political language: spin doctor, soundbite, manifesto, campaign, propaganda, rhetoric, partisan, mandate, poll, approval rating. Critical questions: Who benefits? What is omitted? Is the language emotive? Are claims substantiated?\nVerbs: pledge, vow, denounce, endorse, dismiss. Neutral vs strong reporting verbs: say / state / claim / insist / allege. Reporting: The minister is reported to have said…'],
      eg: [['The minister pledged to cut taxes.', 'Vazir soliqlarni kamaytirishga va’da berdi.'], ['The press denounced the decision.', 'Matbuot qarorni qoraladi.'], ['The poll shows a drop in his approval rating.', 'So‘rov uning ma’qullash darajasi pasayganini ko‘rsatadi.'], ['The minister is reported to have dismissed the criticism.', 'Vazir tanqidni rad etgani xabar qilinadi.']],
      w: [['manifesto', 'dastur (partiya)', 'манифест', 'noun', 'The party published its manifesto.', 'Partiya o‘z dasturini e’lon qildi.', 'ˌmænɪˈfestəʊ'], ['soundbite', 'qisqa iqtibos', 'короткая цитата', 'noun', 'The soundbite went viral.', 'Qisqa iqtibos tez tarqaldi.', 'ˈsaʊndbaɪt'], ['pledge', 'va’da bermoq', 'обещать', 'verb', 'He pledged to reduce costs.', 'U xarajatlarni kamaytirishga va’da berdi.', 'pledʒ'], ['denounce', 'qoralamoq', 'осуждать', 'verb', 'Critics denounced the policy.', 'Tanqidchilar siyosatni qoraladi.', 'dɪˈnaʊns'], ['partisan', 'partiyaviy (tarafdor)', 'пристрастный', 'adjective', 'The report was partisan.', 'Hisobot partiyaviy edi.', 'ˌpɑːtɪˈzæn'], ['rhetoric', 'ritorika', 'риторика', 'noun', 'The rhetoric was powerful.', 'Ritorika kuchli edi.', 'ˈretərɪk']],
      g: [['The minister ___ to cut taxes.', 'pledged', 'pledge', 'pledging', 'O‘tgan zamon.'], ['The minister is reported ___ dismissed the criticism.', 'to have', 'having', 'that he', 'Majhul xabar.']],
      dlg: ['Student: How should I read political news critically? Teacher: Ask who benefits from the story. Student: And language? Teacher: Notice emotive words and soundbites. A partisan newspaper may denounce or endorse depending on its agenda. Student: And claims? Teacher: Check whether they are substantiated with evidence.', 'What should you check about claims?', 'Whether they are substantiated with evidence', 'How long they are', 'Who read them', 'The teacher says so.'],
      rd: ['Politicians know that the media shapes public opinion, so they often prepare short, memorable soundbites designed to be quoted. Spin doctors advise on how to present difficult news in the most favourable light, while campaigns use rhetoric to appeal to emotion. Critical readers therefore examine not only what is said but also what is left out, who is quoted and which words are chosen. Comparing coverage from newspapers with different political leanings is an effective way to detect partisan framing.', ['How can partisan framing be detected?', 'By comparing coverage from different newspapers', 'By reading only one paper', 'By ignoring the words used'], ['Politicians avoid the media.', 'False'], ['Spin doctors are elected officials.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Analyse a short political statement (invented) in 180 words: purpose, language, what is omitted and your evaluation.', 'rhetoric|soundbite|substantiated|partisan', 120, 'The statement “We will cut your taxes and protect your future” is a typical soundbite. Its purpose is to appeal to voters. The rhetoric is emotive, and the claim is not substantiated with figures. The speaker omits how the cuts will be funded. The language is partisan, aimed at supporters. A critical reader should look for evidence and compare other sources.'],
      sp: ['Analyse the purpose of a campaign slogan in four sentences.', 'The slogan is a soundbite meant to be memorable. It uses emotive language. It does not provide evidence. Readers should check the claims.'],
      ts: [['A short quote designed for the media:', 'soundbite', 'manifesto', 'mandate'], ['“Qoralamoq” in English:', 'denounce', 'pledge', 'endorse'], ['A ___ newspaper supports one party.', 'partisan', 'neutral', 'balanced']] },
  ], exam: [['The law ___ approved by a large majority.', 'was', 'were', 'is being', 'grammar'], ['“Hukm (hay’at)” in English:', 'verdict', 'bail', 'plaintiff', 'vocabulary'], ['The minister is reported ___ resigned.', 'to have', 'having', 'that has', 'grammar']] },

  { title: 'Advanced grammar', lessons: [
    { t: ['Inversion and emphasis', 'Inversiya va ta’kid'],
      obj: ['Inversiya va boshqa ta’kid vositalari bilan nutqni kuchaytirasiz.', 'You will strengthen speech and writing with inversion and other emphatic devices.'],
      ex: ['Inversiya (yordamchi fe’l subyektdan oldin keladi) salbiy yoki cheklovchi ravishlardan keyin: Never have I seen…, Rarely does he…, Not only did she…, Hardly had we arrived when…, No sooner had he left than…, Seldom do people…, Only after… did…, Under no circumstances should you…, At no time was he…\nBoshqa ta’kid: do/does/did + fe’l (I did enjoy it), cleft (What I need is…), Fronting (Away ran the dog), so + sifat + that (So difficult was the exam that…), such + ot (Such was his anger that…). Rasmiy va adabiy uslub.', 'Inversion (auxiliary before subject) after negative or restrictive adverbials: Never have I seen…, Rarely does he…, Not only did she…, Hardly had we arrived when…, No sooner had he left than…, Seldom do people…, Only after… did…, Under no circumstances should you…, At no time was he…\nOther emphasis: do/does/did + verb (I did enjoy it), cleft (What I need is…), fronting (Away ran the dog), so + adjective + that (So difficult was the exam that…), such + noun (Such was his anger that…). Formal and literary style.'],
      eg: [['Never have I seen such a beautiful city.', 'Hech qachon bunday go‘zal shaharni ko‘rmaganman.'], ['Hardly had we arrived when it started to rain.', 'Yetib kelishimiz bilanoq yomg‘ir boshlandi.'], ['Under no circumstances should you open this door.', 'Hech qanday sharoitda bu eshikni ochmang.'], ['So difficult was the exam that few students passed.', 'Imtihon shu qadar qiyin ediki, kam talaba o‘tdi.']],
      w: [['seldom', 'kamdan-kam', 'редко', 'adverb', 'Seldom do we see snow here.', 'Bu yerda kamdan-kam qor ko‘ramiz.', 'ˈseldəm'], ['hardly', 'zo‘rg‘a, deyarli', 'едва', 'adverb', 'Hardly anyone knew the answer.', 'Deyarli hech kim javobni bilmasdi.', 'ˈhɑːdli'], ['circumstance', 'sharoit', 'обстоятельство', 'noun', 'Under no circumstances should you panic.', 'Hech qanday sharoitda vahima qilmang.', 'ˈsɜːkəmstəns'], ['no sooner', 'shundan keyin darhol', 'как только', 'phrase', 'No sooner had he left than it rained.', 'U ketishi bilan yomg‘ir yog‘di.', 'nəʊ ˈsuːnə'], ['emphasis', 'ta’kid', 'акцент', 'noun', 'Add emphasis with inversion.', 'Inversiya bilan ta’kid qo‘shing.', 'ˈemfəsɪs'], ['scarcely', 'zo‘rg‘a (formal)', 'едва ли', 'adverb', 'Scarcely had he spoken when she left.', 'U gapirib bo‘lishi bilan u ketdi.', 'ˈskeəsli']],
      g: [['Never ___ I seen such a beautiful city.', 'have', 'had to', 'did', 'Inversiya: Never have I seen.'], ['Hardly had we arrived ___ it started to rain.', 'when', 'than', 'as', '“Hardly … when”.']],
      dlg: ['Teacher: Rewrite: “I have never seen such a city.” Student: Never have I seen such a city. Teacher: Now: “He had just left when it rained.” Student: No sooner had he left than it rained. Teacher: Excellent. Remember: only after negative or restrictive adverbs does inversion occur.', 'When does inversion occur?', 'After negative or restrictive adverbials', 'After every verb', 'Only in questions', 'The teacher says so.'],
      rd: ['Inversion is one of the most striking features of formal and literary English. When a sentence begins with a negative adverbial such as “never”, “rarely” or “not only”, the auxiliary verb comes before the subject: “Rarely does a film move me so deeply.” This word order creates emphasis and sounds elevated, so it is common in speeches and academic writing but less so in everyday conversation. Used sparingly, it adds variety; used too often, it can make prose sound artificial.', ['Why is inversion used?', 'To create emphasis', 'To shorten the sentence', 'To avoid verbs'], ['Inversion is typical of casual speech.', 'False'], ['Inversion exists only in English.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Rewrite 8 sentences using inversion and write 4 original sentences with emphatic structures.', 'never have|hardly had|not only|under no circumstances', 80, 'Never have I seen such a beautiful city. Hardly had we arrived when it began to rain. Not only did she win, but she also broke the record. Under no circumstances should you open this door. So difficult was the exam that few students passed.'],
      sp: ['Say three emphatic sentences with inversion about a memorable experience.', 'Never have I felt so proud. Hardly had I entered the hall when everyone applauded. Rarely do I feel so happy.'],
      ts: [['Inversion: “I have never seen it.”', 'Never have I seen it.', 'Never I have seen it.', 'I never have seen it.'], ['“Kamdan-kam” in English:', 'seldom', 'hardly', 'scarcely'], ['No sooner had he left ___ it rained.', 'than', 'when', 'then']] },
    { t: ['Mixed conditionals and the subjunctive', 'Aralash shartli va subjunktiv'],
      obj: ['Aralash shartli gaplar va subjunktivni aniq va uslubiy to‘g‘ri qo‘llaysiz.', 'You will use mixed conditionals and the subjunctive accurately and stylistically.'],
      ex: ['Aralash shartli: o‘tgan sabab → hozirgi natija: If I had taken that job, I would be living abroad now. Hozirgi holat → o‘tgan natija: If she were more careful, she wouldn’t have made that mistake. Inversiya: Had I known, … ; Were I you, … ; Should you need help, …\nSubjunktiv (rasmiy): suggest / insist / demand / recommend + that + asosiy shakl: I suggest that he be present. It is essential that she arrive on time. Qaror: If only / I wish + Past Perfect; as if / as though + Past (haqiqatga zid): He talks as if he knew everything. Oldingi ibora: Suppose / supposing, otherwise, but for.', 'Mixed conditional: past cause → present result: If I had taken that job, I would be living abroad now. Present state → past result: If she were more careful, she wouldn’t have made that mistake. Inversion: Had I known, … ; Were I you, … ; Should you need help, …\nSubjunctive (formal): suggest / insist / demand / recommend + that + base form: I suggest that he be present. It is essential that she arrive on time. Wishes: If only / I wish + Past Perfect; as if / as though + Past (unreal): He talks as if he knew everything. Others: suppose / supposing, otherwise, but for.'],
      eg: [['If I had taken that job, I would be living abroad now.', 'O‘sha ishni olganimda, hozir chet elda yashagan bo‘lardim.'], ['If she were more careful, she wouldn’t have made that mistake.', 'U ehtiyotkorroq bo‘lganida, bu xatoni qilmagan bo‘lardi.'], ['It is essential that she arrive on time.', 'Uning o‘z vaqtida yetib kelishi zarur.'], ['He talks as if he knew everything.', 'U hamma narsani biladigandek gapiradi.']],
      w: [['subjunctive', 'subjunktiv (shart mayli)', 'сослагательное наклонение', 'noun', 'The subjunctive is formal.', 'Subjunktiv rasmiy.', 'səbˈdʒʌŋktɪv'], ['insist', 'turib olmoq', 'настаивать', 'verb', 'She insisted that he apologise.', 'U uzr so‘rashini talab qildi.', 'ɪnˈsɪst'], ['demand', 'talab qilmoq', 'требовать', 'verb', 'They demanded that he resign.', 'Ular uning iste’foga chiqishini talab qildi.', 'dɪˈmɑːnd'], ['essential', 'zarur', 'необходимый', 'adjective', 'It is essential that you attend.', 'Qatnashishingiz zarur.', 'ɪˈsenʃl'], ['supposing', 'faraz qilaylik', 'предположим', 'conjunction', 'Supposing it rains, what then?', 'Faraz qilaylik, yomg‘ir yog‘sa, nima bo‘ladi?', 'səˈpəʊzɪŋ'], ['regretful', 'afsuslanuvchi', 'сожалеющий', 'adjective', 'He felt regretful.', 'U afsuslandi.', 'rɪˈɡretfl']],
      g: [['If I had taken that job, I ___ abroad now.', 'would be living', 'would have lived', 'will live', 'Aralash: o‘tgan sabab → hozirgi natija.'], ['It is essential that she ___ on time.', 'arrive', 'arrives', 'arrived', 'Subjunktiv: asosiy shakl.']],
      dlg: ['Teacher: Complete: If I had studied medicine, I … a doctor now. Student: would be. Teacher: And: If he were more careful, he … that mistake. Student: wouldn’t have made. Teacher: Good. Now the subjunctive: It is essential that she … on time. Student: arrive.', 'What verb form follows “It is essential that”?', 'The base form', 'The -s form', 'The past form', 'The student says “arrive”.'],
      rd: ['Mixed conditionals link different time frames: a hypothetical past action may have a result that continues into the present, or a present characteristic may explain a past outcome. “If I had accepted the offer, I would be a manager now” is a typical example. The subjunctive, rare in everyday English, appears in formal contexts after verbs such as “suggest”, “insist” and “recommend”: “The committee recommends that the proposal be rejected.” Mastering both structures is a mark of advanced grammatical control.', ['Where does the subjunctive appear?', 'After verbs like suggest, insist and recommend in formal contexts', 'In every sentence', 'Only in questions'], ['Mixed conditionals use one time frame only.', 'False'], ['The subjunctive is common in slang.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Write 8 sentences about missed opportunities and formal recommendations using mixed conditionals and the subjunctive.', 'would be|wouldn’t have|it is essential that|as if', 80, 'If I had accepted the offer, I would be a manager now. If she were more careful, she wouldn’t have made that mistake. The committee recommends that the proposal be rejected. It is essential that every participant arrive on time. He behaves as if he owned the company.'],
      sp: ['Say one mixed conditional about your life and one formal recommendation.', 'If I had studied abroad, I would be working there now. I recommend that the plan be approved.'],
      ts: [['Subjunctive: “I suggest that he ___ present.”', 'be', 'is', 'was'], ['“Talab qilmoq” in English:', 'demand', 'insist', 'suppose'], ['If I ___ rich, I would have helped him.', 'were', 'am', 'was being']] },
    { t: ['Cleft sentences and complex noun phrases', 'Cleft gaplar va murakkab ot birikmalari'],
      obj: ['Cleft gaplar va murakkab ot birikmalari bilan axborotni zichlashtirasiz.', 'You will condense information with cleft sentences and complex noun phrases.'],
      ex: ['Cleft gaplar fokusni siljitadi: It-cleft: It was the manager who made the decision. Wh-cleft: What I need is more time. All-cleft: All we want is peace. Reverse wh-cleft: More time is what I need. Maqsad: ta’kid, kontrast. There-cleft: There is something I need to say.\nMurakkab ot birikmasi: premodifikatsiya (a recently published, widely cited study), postmodifikatsiya (the rise in prices over the last decade which has affected families), nominalizatsiya (to fail → failure; to decide → decision). Zichlik: rasmiy yozuvda ma’lumot ko‘p, fe’l kam. Misol: The company’s sudden decision to close the factory caused widespread concern.', 'Cleft sentences shift the focus. It-cleft: It was the manager who made the decision. Wh-cleft: What I need is more time. All-cleft: All we want is peace. Reverse wh-cleft: More time is what I need. Purpose: emphasis, contrast. There-cleft: There is something I need to say.\nComplex noun phrases: premodification (a recently published, widely cited study), postmodification (the rise in prices over the last decade which has affected families), nominalisation (to fail → failure; to decide → decision). Density: formal writing packs information into nouns. Example: The company’s sudden decision to close the factory caused widespread concern.'],
      eg: [['It was the manager who made the decision.', 'Qarorni aynan menejer qabul qildi.'], ['What I need is more time.', 'Menga kerak narsa — ko‘proq vaqt.'], ['The company’s sudden decision to close the factory caused widespread concern.', 'Kompaniyaning zavodni yopish haqidagi to‘satdan qarori keng xavotir uyg‘otdi.'], ['All we want is peace.', 'Biz faqat tinchlikni istaymiz.']],
      w: [['cleft', 'ajratilgan (gap)', 'расщеплённое предложение', 'adjective', 'A cleft sentence adds focus.', 'Cleft gap fokus qo‘shadi.', 'kleft'], ['premodifier', 'old aniqlovchi', 'определение перед существительным', 'noun', 'An adjective is a premodifier.', 'Sifat — old aniqlovchi.', 'ˌpriːˈmɒdɪfaɪə'], ['postmodifier', 'keyingi aniqlovchi', 'определение после существительного', 'noun', 'A relative clause is a postmodifier.', 'Aniqlovchi gap — keyingi aniqlovchi.', 'ˌpəʊstˈmɒdɪfaɪə'], ['condense', 'zichlashtirmoq', 'уплотнять', 'verb', 'Condense the information.', 'Ma’lumotni zichlashtiring.', 'kənˈdens'], ['widespread', 'keng tarqalgan', 'широко распространённый', 'adjective', 'There was widespread concern.', 'Keng tarqalgan xavotir bor edi.', 'ˈwaɪdspred'], ['nominal', 'otga oid', 'именной', 'adjective', 'A nominal group centres on a noun.', 'Ot birikmasi otga tayanadi.', 'ˈnɒmɪnl']],
      g: [['It was the manager ___ made the decision.', 'who', 'which', 'whose', 'Odam — who.'], ['___ I need is more time.', 'What', 'That', 'Which', 'Wh-cleft.']],
      dlg: ['Teacher: Turn this into a cleft: “The manager made the decision.” Student: It was the manager who made the decision. Teacher: And a wh-cleft: “I need more time.” Student: What I need is more time. Teacher: Now condense: “The company decided suddenly to close the factory.” Student: The company’s sudden decision to close the factory.', 'What did the student create with “sudden decision”?', 'A noun phrase with a nominalisation', 'A question', 'A cleft sentence', 'It is a complex noun phrase.'],
      rd: ['The sudden closure of the region’s largest factory, announced without warning late last Friday, has left more than two thousand workers facing an uncertain future. It is the lack of consultation that has angered unions most. What the workers want is clarity about redundancy payments and retraining schemes. The managing director defended the decision, insisting that the long-term viability of the company depended on cutting costs, but critics argue that the human cost has been seriously underestimated.', ['What has angered unions most?', 'The lack of consultation', 'The factory’s size', 'The redundancy payment amount'], ['The factory was closed after consultation.', 'False'], ['The workers will be transferred abroad.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Rewrite a short news paragraph (6 sentences) using cleft sentences and complex noun phrases, then compare the two versions.', 'it was|what|the sudden|decision to', 90, 'It was the lack of consultation that angered the unions. What workers want is clarity about redundancy payments. The company’s sudden decision to close the factory caused widespread concern. All they ask for is fair treatment. The announcement, made late on Friday, shocked employees.'],
      sp: ['Say three cleft sentences about your priorities.', 'What I value most is honesty. It is time that matters to me. All I need is a quiet place to study.'],
      ts: [['Cleft sentence:', 'What I need is more time.', 'I need more time.', 'More I need time.'], ['“Zichlashtirmoq” in English:', 'condense', 'cleft', 'nominal'], ['It was the lack of consultation ___ angered them.', 'that', 'what', 'whose']] },
    { t: ['C1 review: precision and style', 'C1 takrorlash: aniqlik va uslub'],
      obj: ['C1 mavzularini birlashtirib, aniq, uslubiy to‘g‘ri va dalilli matn yaratasiz.', 'You will combine C1 topics to produce precise, stylistically accurate and well-argued texts.'],
      ex: ['Takrorlash: dalil qurilishi (claim–premise–evidence–warrant), qarshi dalil, hedging, konnotatsiya va ohang, akademik uslub, parafraz, ma’lumotni tasvirlash, bioetika va mas’uliyat tillari, inversiya, aralash shartli, cleft, murakkab ot birikmasi. Aniqlik: umumiy so‘zdan (thing, good, bad) saqlaning → aniq so‘zlar: initiative, beneficial, detrimental. Uslub: sintaktik xilma-xillik (qisqa va uzun gaplar), ritm, so‘z tartibi.\nSamoni tekshirish: 1) Da’vo aniqmi? 2) Dalil bormi? 3) Hedging to‘g‘rimi? 4) Uslub (rasmiy) izchilmi? 5) Grammatik xilma-xillik? 6) Takrorlanishlar kamaydimi?',  'Review: argument construction (claim–premise–evidence–warrant), counterargument, hedging, connotation and tone, academic style, paraphrasing, describing data, language of bioethics and responsibility, inversion, mixed conditionals, cleft sentences, complex noun phrases. Precision: avoid general words (thing, good, bad) → precise words: initiative, beneficial, detrimental. Style: syntactic variety (short and long sentences), rhythm, word order.\nSelf-check: 1) Is the claim clear? 2) Is there evidence? 3) Is hedging appropriate? 4) Is the formal style consistent? 5) Grammatical variety? 6) Repetition reduced?'],
      eg: [['The proposal is potentially beneficial, albeit costly.', 'Taklif potensial foydali, garchi qimmat.'], ['Rarely do policies achieve every goal, which suggests that trade-offs are inevitable.', 'Siyosatlar kamdan-kam hamma maqsadga erishadi, bu murosalar muqarrarligini ko‘rsatadi.'], ['What the evidence indicates is a gradual decline.', 'Dalillar ko‘rsatayotgan narsa — asta-sekin pasayish.'], ['Had governments acted sooner, the impact would have been limited.', 'Hukumatlar tezroq harakat qilganida, ta’sir cheklangan bo‘lardi.']],
      w: [['precision', 'aniqlik', 'точность', 'noun', 'Precision matters in academic writing.', 'Akademik yozuvda aniqlik muhim.', 'prɪˈsɪʒn'], ['inevitable', 'muqarrar', 'неизбежный', 'adjective', 'Change is inevitable.', 'O‘zgarish muqarrar.', 'ɪnˈevɪtəbl'], ['beneficial', 'foydali', 'полезный', 'adjective', 'The reform is beneficial.', 'Islohot foydali.', 'ˌbenɪˈfɪʃl'], ['detrimental', 'zararli', 'вредный', 'adjective', 'It is detrimental to health.', 'Bu sog‘liq uchun zararli.', 'ˌdetrɪˈmentl'], ['stylistic', 'uslubiy', 'стилистический', 'adjective', 'Make stylistic choices carefully.', 'Uslubiy tanlovlarni ehtiyotkor qiling.', 'staɪˈlɪstɪk'], ['variety', 'xilma-xillik', 'разнообразие', 'noun', 'Use variety in sentence length.', 'Gap uzunligida xilma-xillikdan foydalaning.', 'vəˈraɪəti']],
      g: [['Had governments acted sooner, the impact ___ been limited.', 'would have', 'will have', 'had', 'Third conditional inversiya.'], ['The proposal is beneficial, ___ costly.', 'albeit', 'albeing', 'because', '“albeit” + sifat.']],
      dlg: ['Tutor: Check your essay: is the claim clear? Student: I think so. Tutor: Is there evidence and a counterargument? Student: Yes, and I hedged carefully. Tutor: What about variety in structures? Student: I used inversion and a cleft sentence. Tutor: Replace “good” with “beneficial” and you have a C1 style.', 'What should the student replace “good” with?', 'Beneficial', 'Bad', 'Nice', 'The tutor says “beneficial”.'],
      rd: ['At C1 level, the difference between a good and an excellent text often lies in precision and control. Excellent writers choose exact words instead of general ones, vary sentence length for rhythm and use advanced structures only where they add meaning. They state a clear position, support it with evidence, acknowledge counterarguments and qualify claims appropriately. Above all, they revise: reading their draft critically, removing repetition and checking that style remains consistent from the first sentence to the last.', ['What do excellent writers do above all?', 'Revise their drafts critically', 'Use only long words', 'Avoid counterarguments'], ['Excellent writers use general words.', 'False'], ['The text was written by a teacher.', 'Not Given']],
      wr: ['Write a final C1 essay (300 words): “Technology, not government policy, will solve the climate crisis.” Discuss and give your opinion, using a clear thesis, evidence, a counterargument, hedging and at least two advanced grammatical structures.', 'it could be argued|albeit|had|rarely do', 220, 'It could be argued that technology, rather than government policy, will solve the climate crisis. This essay will argue that, while technology is essential, it cannot succeed without policy support. Renewable energy has become markedly cheaper, albeit unevenly distributed. Rarely do markets alone, however, drive such change at the necessary speed. Had governments acted sooner, the impact would have been more limited. What is needed is a combination of innovation and regulation. Ultimately, technology and policy are complementary rather than competing.'],
      sp: ['Speak for two minutes: take a position on a current issue, give evidence, acknowledge an objection and conclude.', 'I believe that remote work is beneficial. Evidence suggests that productivity rises. Admittedly, collaboration may suffer. Nevertheless, a hybrid model offers the best balance.'],
      ts: [['Replace “bad” with a precise word:', 'detrimental', 'good', 'nice'], ['“Muqarrar” in English:', 'inevitable', 'stylistic', 'variety'], ['Had I known, I ___ helped.', 'would have', 'will have', 'did']] },
  ], exam: [['Never ___ I seen such a view.', 'have', 'had to', 'did', 'grammar'], ['It is vital that every candidate ___ before nine.', 'arrive', 'arrives', 'arrived', 'grammar'], ['It was the delay ___ caused the problem.', 'that', 'what', 'whose', 'grammar']] },
];
